---
title: Query Collection
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query collections provide seamless integration between TanStack DB and TanStack Query, enabling automatic synchronization between your local database and remote data sources."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Overview"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/query-db-collection"},{"type":"text","value":" package allows you to create collections that:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatically fetch remote data via TanStack Query"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Support optimistic updates with automatic rollback on errors"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handle persistence through customizable mutation handlers"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provide direct write capabilities for directly writing to the sync store"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Installation"}]},{"type":"code","value":"npm install @tanstack/query-db-collection @tanstack/query-core @tanstack/db","lang":"bash"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"basic-usage","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic Usage"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { QueryClient } from \"@tanstack/query-core\"\nimport { DbClient, collectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/db\"\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\"\n\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient()\nconst db = new DbClient({ queryClient })\n\nconst todosCollection = collectionOptions(\"todos\", (client) =>\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    id: \"todos\",\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async () => {\n      const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\")\n      return response.json()\n    },\n    queryClient: client.requireDependency<QueryClient>(\"queryClient\"),\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  })\n)\n\nconst todos = db.collection(todosCollection)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"configuration-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration Options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryCollectionOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" function accepts the following options:"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"required-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Required Options"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryKey"},{"type":"text","value":": The query key for TanStack Query. Can be a static array or a function that receives "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"LoadSubsetOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" and returns a key. When using a function, all returned keys must share the base key ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryKey({})"},{"type":"text","value":") as a prefix — see "},{"type":"link","href":"#query-key-prefix-convention","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query Key Prefix Convention"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":": Function that fetches data from the server"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryClient"},{"type":"text","value":": TanStack Query client instance"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getKey"},{"type":"text","value":": Function to extract the unique key from an item"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"request-scoped-queryclient","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request-scoped QueryClient"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryCollectionOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" needs a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryClient"},{"type":"text","value":". In SSR, TanStack Start, tests,\nor multi-tenant apps, that client is request-local rather than module-global.\nPut it on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DbClient"},{"type":"text","value":", then resolve it inside the collection descriptor factory:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { QueryClient } from \"@tanstack/query-core\"\nimport { DbClient, collectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/db\"\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\"\n\ninterface Todo {\n  id: string\n  title: string\n}\n\nexport const todoCollection = collectionOptions(\"todos\", (client) =>\n  queryCollectionOptions<Todo>({\n    id: \"todos\",\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async () => {\n      const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\")\n      return response.json() as Promise<Array<Todo>>\n    },\n    queryClient: client.requireDependency<QueryClient>(\"queryClient\"),\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n  })\n)\n\nexport function createRequestClients() {\n  const queryClient = new QueryClient()\n  const dbClient = new DbClient({ queryClient })\n  return { queryClient, dbClient }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dbClient.collection(todoCollection)"},{"type":"text","value":" memoizes one collection instance for that\ndescriptor and client. A second "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DbClient"},{"type":"text","value":" materializes fresh adapter state and\nuses its own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryClient"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Passing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryClient"},{"type":"text","value":" directly to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryCollectionOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" remains supported for\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCollection(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" and existing apps. When a descriptor is materialized,\nan explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DbClient"},{"type":"text","value":" dependency takes precedence; the configured\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryClient"},{"type":"text","value":" is the backwards-compatible fallback."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"business-scoped-collection-factories","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Business-Scoped Collection Factories"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A tenant, project, account, or route parameter can define a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"business scope"}]},{"type":"text","value":":\nthe server resource that a collection represents. Include the scope in the\ndescriptor id, Query key, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":". This extends the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"#request-scoped-queryclient","children":[{"type":"text","value":"request-scoped QueryClient pattern"}]},{"type":"text","value":" with an\nexplicit scope parameter:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface Todo {\n  id: string\n  title: string\n  projectId: string\n}\n\nasync function fetchProjectTodos(projectId: string): Promise<Array<Todo>> {\n  const response = await fetch(`/api/projects/${projectId}/todos`)\n  return response.json()\n}\n\nfunction createProjectTodosDescriptor(\n  projectId: string,\n) {\n  return collectionOptions(`project:${projectId}:todos`, (client) =>\n    queryCollectionOptions<Todo>({\n      id: `project:${projectId}:todos`,\n      queryKey: [\"projects\", projectId, \"todos\"],\n      queryFn: () => fetchProjectTodos(projectId),\n      queryClient: client.requireDependency<QueryClient>(\"queryClient\"),\n      getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    })\n  )\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The scope is part of the descriptor identity. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DbClient"},{"type":"text","value":" resolves separately\ncreated descriptors with the same id to the same collection, so a React hook\ncan create the descriptor from its current parameters:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export function useProjectTodos(projectId: string) {\n  return useDbClient().collection(createProjectTodosDescriptor(projectId))\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Only the first descriptor for an id is materialized. Include every scope value\nthat changes the collection in both its descriptor id and Query key. Call\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"await dbClient.cleanup()"},{"type":"text","value":" when the client scope ends."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A business scope is separate from a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"relational subset"}]},{"type":"text","value":" requested by a live query. With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncMode: \"on-demand\""},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"LoadSubsetOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" describes predicates, ordering, limits, and offsets within one business-scoped collection. These options reach "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" and determine the subset Query keys. See "},{"type":"link","href":"#queryfn-and-predicate-push-down","children":[{"type":"text","value":"QueryFn and Predicate Push-Down"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not create a collection for each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"where"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"orderBy"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"limit"},{"type":"text","value":". Reuse the business-scoped collection and let on-demand loading represent those subsets. Create separate collections only for distinct server resources."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"query-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query Options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query Collections use TanStack Query internally and expose supported Query observer options as top-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryCollectionOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" fields."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The following top-level Query Collection options are forwarded to the underlying Query observer:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":": Function that extracts the row array TanStack DB materializes from a wrapped Query response"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether the query should automatically run (default: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetchInterval"},{"type":"text","value":": Refetch interval in milliseconds"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"retry"},{"type":"text","value":": Retry configuration for failed queries"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"retryDelay"},{"type":"text","value":": Delay between retries"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"staleTime"},{"type":"text","value":": How long data is considered fresh"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gcTime"},{"type":"text","value":": How long unused query data stays in the Query cache"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetchOnWindowFocus"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether to refetch when the window regains focus"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetchOnReconnect"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether to refetch when the network reconnects"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetchOnMount"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether to refetch when the observer mounts"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"networkMode"},{"type":"text","value":": Query network mode"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":": Initial Query response for eager collections"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialDataUpdatedAt"},{"type":"text","value":": Timestamp used by TanStack Query to determine initial data freshness"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":": Metadata passed to the query function context. Query Collections may add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadSubsetOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" for on-demand queries."}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"const todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: fetchTodos,\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    refetchOnWindowFocus: true,\n    refetchOnReconnect: true,\n    refetchOnMount: \"always\",\n    networkMode: \"online\",\n  })\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Top-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" is always merged by Query Collection so it can add on-demand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadSubsetOptions"},{"type":"text","value":". Other supported top-level Query options are only passed to TanStack Query when you define them. If you omit them, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryClient.defaultOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" can still apply."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Some fields are owned or reinterpreted by the collection adapter rather than treated as ordinary Query option pass-through:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryKey"},{"type":"text","value":": Identifies the Query cache entry and, in on-demand mode, may be built from load-subset options."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":": Fetches the complete collection state or the requested on-demand subset."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":": Extracts array rows from wrapped responses before they are stored in the collection. This is not the same contract as TanStack Query's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" option."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryClient"},{"type":"text","value":": Supplies the Query client instance used by the collection."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncMode"},{"type":"text","value":": Controls whether the collection syncs eagerly or on demand."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getKey"},{"type":"text","value":": Extracts each row's stable TanStack DB key."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutation handlers such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onInsert"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUpdate"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onDelete"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Some TanStack Query fields are owned or reinterpreted by Query Collection and are intentionally not exposed as ordinary Query observer options:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryKey"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryClient"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" (Query Collection uses this for row extraction, not TanStack Query's observer-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" contract)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" (merged by Query Collection so on-demand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadSubsetOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" can be included)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subscribed"},{"type":"text","value":" (Query Collection owns the observer subscription lifecycle)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structuralSharing"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"notifyOnChangeProps"},{"type":"text","value":" (managed by Query Collection synchronization)"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placeholderData"},{"type":"text","value":" is intentionally unsupported. TanStack Query treats placeholder data as observer-local presentation state rather than cached Query data. Materializing it would expose temporary UI data as collection-wide normalized rows. Render placeholders in the consuming UI instead."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"request-cancellation-with-queryfunctioncontext-signal","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request Cancellation with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryFunctionContext.signal"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query passes an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortSignal"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" through the query function\ncontext. Forward "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.signal"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"},{"type":"text","value":" or another abortable client to make the\nrequest cancellable:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\", {\n        signal: ctx.signal,\n      })\n\n      if (!response.ok) {\n        throw new Error(\"Failed to fetch todos\")\n      }\n\n      return response.json() as Promise<Array<Todo>>\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n  }),\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explicit collection cleanup cancels each exact Query key the collection is\ncurrently tracking before removing it from the Query cache:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"await todosCollection.cleanup()","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The underlying request is aborted only when its client consumes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.signal"},{"type":"text","value":".\nA client that ignores the signal may continue its request even though the\ncollection has been cleaned up."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An unloaded on-demand subset is no longer tracked. A later explicit collection\ncleanup does not revisit its Query key."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query cache entries are shared within a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryClient"},{"type":"text","value":". Explicit cleanup can\naffect other consumers using the same exact Query keys."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On-demand subset unloading does not explicitly call\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryClient.cancelQueries()"},{"type":"text","value":". It removes the subset's Query observer. If this\nwas the final observer and the query function consumed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.signal"},{"type":"text","value":", TanStack\nQuery aborts the request. If the signal was ignored, or another observer still\nuses the same exact Query key, the request may finish and remain cached until\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gcTime"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"using-with-queryoptions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryOptions(...)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your app already uses TanStack Query's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" helper (e.g. from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/react-query"},{"type":"text","value":"), you can spread compatible top-level options into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryCollectionOptions"},{"type":"text","value":". Note that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" must be explicitly provided since query collections require it both in types and at runtime, and Query Collection's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" option is for row extraction rather than TanStack Query observer-level selection:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { QueryClient } from \"@tanstack/query-core\"\nimport { DbClient, collectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/db\"\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\"\nimport { queryOptions } from \"@tanstack/react-query\"\n\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient()\nconst db = new DbClient({ queryClient })\n\nconst listOptions = queryOptions({\n  queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n  queryFn: async () => {\n    const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\")\n    return response.json() as Promise<Array<{ id: string; title: string }>>\n  },\n})\n\nconst todosCollection = collectionOptions(\"todos\", (client) =>\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    id: \"todos\",\n    ...listOptions,\n    queryFn: (context) => listOptions.queryFn!(context),\n    queryClient: client.requireDependency<QueryClient>(\"queryClient\"),\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  }),\n)\n\nconst todos = db.collection(todosCollection)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" is missing at runtime, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryCollectionOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" throws "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryFnRequiredError"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"initial-data","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initial Data"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Eager Query Collections support TanStack Query's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialDataUpdatedAt"},{"type":"text","value":" options. Initial data has the original Query response\nshape, is stored in the Query cache, and is immediately materialized as\nnormalized collection rows. TanStack Query uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialDataUpdatedAt"},{"type":"text","value":" together\nwith "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"staleTime"},{"type":"text","value":" to decide whether to fetch."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const serverRenderedAt = Date.now()\nconst initialTodos = [\n  { id: \"1\", title: \"Write documentation\" },\n  { id: \"2\", title: \"Ship initial data support\" },\n]\n\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: fetchTodos,\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    initialData: initialTodos,\n    initialDataUpdatedAt: serverRenderedAt,\n    staleTime: 60_000,\n  }),\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An existing cached or hydrated Query response takes precedence over\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":". Query keys remain the cache identity: two collections using the\nsame QueryClient and exact Query key observe one shared Query document, and a\nlater collection's initializer does not replace it. Use distinct Query keys for\nindependent documents."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initial data is supported only for eager collections. A collection-wide value\ncannot establish row membership for arbitrary on-demand predicates, ordering,\nlimits, and offsets. For "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncMode: \"on-demand\""},{"type":"text","value":", seed or hydrate the exact\nderived Query cache entries instead."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If a stale initial response triggers a fetch, the initial rows remain available\nwhile it is in flight. A successful response reconciles them through the normal\nrow ownership pipeline; an error retains the initial rows. Direct writes patch\nthe eager Query cache in place. On-demand direct writes revalidate scoped\nentries as described below."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"selecting-rows-from-wrapped-responses","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Selecting Rows from Wrapped Responses"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Many APIs return rows inside a response envelope that also contains metadata such as pagination cursors, totals, or request information. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" to extract the row array that TanStack DB should materialize:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface TodosResponse {\n  items: Array<{ id: string; title: string }>\n  nextCursor?: string\n  total: number\n}\n\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async (): Promise<TodosResponse> => {\n      const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\")\n      return response.json()\n    },\n    initialData: {\n      items: [{ id: \"1\", title: \"Initial todo\" }],\n      nextCursor: undefined,\n      total: 1,\n    },\n    select: (response) => response.items,\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  }),\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" is a query-db-collection row extraction hook. It tells TanStack DB which rows to materialize while the TanStack Query cache keeps the original query response shape. In the example above, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryClient.getQueryData([\"todos\"])"},{"type":"text","value":" still returns the full "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TodosResponse"},{"type":"text","value":", including "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nextCursor"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"total"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same projection applies to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":": provide the complete response\nenvelope, and Query Collection materializes the rows returned by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" while\npreserving the envelope in the Query cache."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This differs from TanStack Query's observer-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":": query-db-collection uses this option to bridge Query's response object into DB's normalized row store."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In eager mode, direct write utilities such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeInsert"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeUpdate"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeDelete"},{"type":"text","value":" make a best-effort attempt to update the matching row array inside wrapped Query cache entries while preserving wrapper metadata. In on-demand mode, they revalidate active scoped queries and remove inactive or disabled cache entries instead of patching them with the full collection snapshot."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This works automatically for simple wrappers such as:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ data: [...] }"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ items: [...] }"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ results: [...] }"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Derived projections, such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select: (response) => response.edges.map((edge) => edge.node)"},{"type":"text","value":", are read-side row extraction only. query-db-collection cannot generally reconstruct the original response envelope from updated rows. Refetch or invalidate the query if the wrapped cache must exactly reflect direct writes for a derived projection."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"collection-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection Options"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":": Unique identifier for the collection"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"schema"},{"type":"text","value":": Schema for validating items"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sync"},{"type":"text","value":": Custom sync configuration"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startSync"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether to start syncing immediately (default: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"persistence-handlers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistence Handlers"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onInsert"},{"type":"text","value":": Handler called before insert operations"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUpdate"},{"type":"text","value":": Handler called before update operations"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onDelete"},{"type":"text","value":": Handler called before delete operations"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"extending-meta-with-custom-properties","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Extending Meta with Custom Properties"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" option allows you to pass additional metadata to your query function. By default, Query Collections automatically include "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadSubsetOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" in the meta object, which contains filtering, sorting, and pagination options for on-demand queries."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadSubsetOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" is request-scoped. Query functions can read it through\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.meta"},{"type":"text","value":", but it is excluded when Query metadata is serialized for SSR.\nHydrated Query metadata therefore does not contain the server request options;\nnew on-demand requests supply their own options. Other enumerable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":"\nproperties retain TanStack Query's normal dehydration behavior."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Treat "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" and its nested request data as read-only.\nDo not edit expression nodes, ordering options, Dates, byte arrays, or membership\narrays. Build separate API parameters instead. Core retains request data without\ncloning it; changing submitted data can make the request disagree with its cache\nkey. To change a query constant, supply a new value rather than mutating the old\none. Cancellation through the request's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortSignal"},{"type":"text","value":" remains supported."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"type-safe-meta-access","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type-Safe Meta Access"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" property is automatically typed as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"LoadSubsetOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" without requiring any additional imports or type assertions:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { parseLoadSubsetOptions } from \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\"\n\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"products\"],\n    syncMode: \"on-demand\",\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      // ✅ Type-safe access - no @ts-ignore needed!\n      const options = parseLoadSubsetOptions(ctx.meta?.loadSubsetOptions)\n\n      // Use the parsed options to fetch only what you need\n      return api.getProducts(options)\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  })\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"adding-custom-meta-properties","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adding Custom Meta Properties"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can extend the meta type to include your own custom properties using TypeScript's module augmentation:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// In a global type definition file (e.g., types.d.ts or global.d.ts)\ndeclare module \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\" {\n  interface QueryCollectionMeta {\n    // Add your custom properties here\n    userId?: string\n    includeDeleted?: boolean\n    cacheTTL?: number\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once you've extended the interface, your custom properties are fully typed throughout your application:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const collection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      // ✅ Both loadSubsetOptions and custom properties are typed\n      const { loadSubsetOptions, userId, includeDeleted } = ctx.meta\n\n      return api.getTodos({\n        ...parseLoadSubsetOptions(loadSubsetOptions),\n        userId,\n        includeDeleted,\n      })\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    // Pass custom meta alongside Query Collection defaults\n    meta: {\n      userId: \"user-123\",\n      includeDeleted: false,\n    },\n  })\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"important-notes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Important Notes"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The module augmentation pattern follows TanStack Query's official approach for typing meta"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryCollectionMeta"},{"type":"text","value":" is an interface (not a type alias), enabling proper TypeScript declaration merging"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your custom properties are merged with the base "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadSubsetOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" property"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All meta properties must be compatible with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Record<string, unknown>"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The augmentation should be done in a file that's included in your TypeScript compilation"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"example-api-request-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: API Request Context"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A common use case is passing request context to your query function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// types.d.ts\ndeclare module \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\" {\n  interface QueryCollectionMeta {\n    authToken?: string\n    locale?: string\n    version?: string\n  }\n}\n\n// collections.ts\nconst productsCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"products\"],\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      const { loadSubsetOptions, authToken, locale, version } = ctx.meta\n\n      return api.getProducts({\n        ...parseLoadSubsetOptions(loadSubsetOptions),\n        headers: {\n          Authorization: `Bearer ${authToken}`,\n          \"Accept-Language\": locale,\n          \"API-Version\": version,\n        },\n      })\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    meta: {\n      authToken: session.token,\n      locale: \"en-US\",\n      version: \"v1\",\n    },\n  })\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"persistence-handlers-2","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistence Handlers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can define handlers that are called when mutations occur. These handlers persist changes to your backend:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: fetchTodos,\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      const newItems = transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.modified)\n      await api.createTodos(newItems)\n      // Auto-refetch happens after handler completes (pre-1.0 behavior)\n    },\n\n    onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      const updates = transaction.mutations.map((m) => ({\n        id: m.key,\n        changes: m.changes,\n      }))\n      await api.updateTodos(updates)\n    },\n\n    onDelete: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      const ids = transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.key)\n      await api.deleteTodos(ids)\n    },\n  })\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note"}]},{"type":"text","value":": QueryCollection currently auto-refetches after handlers complete. See "},{"type":"link","href":"#controlling-refetch-behavior","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlling Refetch Behavior"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for details on this transitional behavior."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"controlling-refetch-behavior","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlling Refetch Behavior"}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"⚠️ Transitional API"}]},{"type":"text","value":": QueryCollection currently auto-refetches after handlers complete. This behavior is deprecated and will be removed in v1.0. See the migration notes below."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"current-behavior-pre-1-0","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Current Behavior (Pre-1.0)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, QueryCollection automatically refetches after each handler completes. To "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"skip"}]},{"type":"text","value":" auto-refetch, return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ refetch: false }"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n  await api.createTodos(transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.modified))\n\n  // Skip auto-refetch - use this when server doesn't modify the data\n  return { refetch: false }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you don't return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ refetch: false }"},{"type":"text","value":", auto-refetch happens automatically."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"v1-0-behavior-future","children":[{"type":"text","value":"v1.0 Behavior (Future)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In v1.0, auto-refetch will be "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"removed"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Handlers will need to explicitly call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection.utils.refetch()"},{"type":"text","value":" when refetching is needed:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"onInsert: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n  await api.createTodos(transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.modified))\n\n  // Explicitly trigger refetch when you need server state\n  await collection.utils.refetch()\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When no user mutation is persisting and no mutation handler is active, the\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetch()"},{"type":"text","value":" promise resolves after every accepted result from that call has been\napplied to Collection rows. This is also the application barrier when the\nresult has no row diff and therefore emits no change event. Rejection is\nfail-fast: with several tracked Queries, one failure may reject the call while\nindependent work remains pending."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While a user mutation is persisting or a Collection mutation handler is active,\nnormal application is queued behind that transaction. To avoid a circular wait\nor an unrelated persistence delay, all overlapping "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetch()"},{"type":"text","value":" calls resolve at\nthe Query fetch boundary. This rule applies whether the mutation or the refetch\nstarts first. It includes calls through the handler parameter, captured or\nexternal Collection references, manual transaction mutation functions, and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createOptimisticAction"},{"type":"text","value":" mutation functions. Code can await the mutation's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPersisted.promise"},{"type":"text","value":" when it needs the complete transaction and its queued\nCollection application. Utilities that refetch, including "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clearError()"},{"type":"text","value":",\ninherit the same phase boundary."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The handler's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection"},{"type":"text","value":" parameter and every matching\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transaction.mutations[n].collection"},{"type":"text","value":" alias preserve the external Collection\nidentity. Strict equality, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Map"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WeakMap"},{"type":"text","value":" lookups therefore continue to\nwork in shared persistence handlers. Phase tracking changes the refetch wait\nboundary without replacing the Collection, transaction, or mutation objects."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"At the application boundary, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" applies to both Query fetch errors\nand errors applying an accepted result to Collection rows. With\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError: true"},{"type":"text","value":", application failure or cancellation rejects the call;\ncancellation uses an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortError"},{"type":"text","value":". With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError: false"},{"type":"text","value":" (the default),\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetch()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns its Query results instead. At the mutation-phase fetch\nboundary, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" applies only to the Query fetch because application\nhappens after that call settles. A later application failure is recorded by the\nCollection's error utilities."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To skip refetch in v1.0, simply don't call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetch()"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n  await api.createTodos(transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.modified))\n\n  // No refetch call = no refetch (v1.0 behavior)\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"when-to-skip-refetch","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to Skip Refetch"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Skip refetching when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You're confident the server state exactly matches what you sent (no server-side processing)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You're handling state updates through other mechanisms (like WebSockets or direct writes)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want to optimize for fewer network requests"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"utility-methods","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Utility Methods"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The collection provides these utility methods via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection.utils"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetch(opts?)"},{"type":"text","value":": Refetch every tracked Query and await the applicable fetch or application boundary"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"opts.throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether Query fetch or Collection application errors reject an application-boundary call (default: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":"); mutation-phase calls cover fetch errors only"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bypasses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled: false"},{"type":"text","value":" to support imperative/manual refetching patterns (similar to hook "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetch()"},{"type":"text","value":" behavior)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Returns the tracked Queries' "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryObserverResult"},{"type":"text","value":" values in tracked-key order"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserves an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" slot if a tracked Query is removed while the refetch starts"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clearError()"},{"type":"text","value":": Refetch with errors enabled; retain the recorded error until a successful result applies to the Collection"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Uses the same phase-dependent boundary as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetch()"},{"type":"text","value":". During a mutation handler, the promise can resolve at the fetch boundary before the result applies and clears the error."}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"direct-writes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Direct Writes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Direct writes are intended for scenarios where the normal query/mutation flow doesn't fit your needs. They write directly to the synced data store and bypass the optimistic update system. Their Query cache behavior depends on the collection's sync mode."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"understanding-the-data-stores","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Understanding the Data Stores"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query Collections maintain two data stores:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Synced Data Store"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - The authoritative state synchronized with the server via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optimistic Mutations Store"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Temporary changes that are applied optimistically before server confirmation"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Normal collection operations (insert, update, delete) create optimistic mutations that are:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applied immediately to the UI"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sent to the server via persistence handlers"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rolled back automatically if the server request fails"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Replaced with server data when the query refetches"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Direct writes bypass this system entirely and write directly to the synced data store, making them useful for handling real-time updates from alternative sources. Active on-demand queries still refetch so each scoped cache remains authoritative for its own request."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"when-to-use-direct-writes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to Use Direct Writes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Direct writes should be used when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You need to sync real-time updates from WebSockets or server-sent events"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You're dealing with large datasets where refetching everything is too expensive"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You receive incremental updates or server-computed field updates"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You need to implement complex pagination or partial data loading scenarios"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"individual-write-operations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Individual Write Operations"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Insert a new item directly to the synced data store\ntodosCollection.utils.writeInsert({\n  id: \"1\",\n  text: \"Buy milk\",\n  completed: false,\n})\n\n// Update an existing item in the synced data store\ntodosCollection.utils.writeUpdate({ id: \"1\", completed: true })\n\n// Delete an item from the synced data store\ntodosCollection.utils.writeDelete(\"1\")\n\n// Upsert (insert or update) in the synced data store\ntodosCollection.utils.writeUpsert({\n  id: \"1\",\n  text: \"Buy milk\",\n  completed: false,\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These operations:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Write directly to the synced data store"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do NOT create optimistic mutations"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Return a promise that resolves when the sync commit is accepted, including any configured persistence write. Await it in a mutation handler before returning so the server response is stored before the optimistic state drops."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While a mutation handler is running, wait behind it and become visible when its optimistic transaction settles, in the same update that drops its optimistic state"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In eager mode, update the full-result TanStack Query cache in place without refetching"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In on-demand mode, refetch active enabled queries and remove inactive or disabled cache entries"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The promise does not wait for on-demand Query revalidation. If a handler returns before it writes the server response, its optimistic state drops and the row shows the previous server value until the response arrives.\nAfter Collection cleanup starts, direct writes fail with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SyncNotInitializedError"},{"type":"text","value":" until a new sync run starts."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"batch-operations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Batch Operations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeBatch"},{"type":"text","value":" method allows you to perform multiple operations atomically. Any write operations called within the callback will be collected and executed as a single transaction:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The batch includes writes to this Collection only. A synchronous callback may also write to another Collection, but that write has its own completion promise."}]},{"type":"code","value":"todosCollection.utils.writeBatch(() => {\n  todosCollection.utils.writeInsert({ id: \"1\", text: \"Buy milk\" })\n  todosCollection.utils.writeInsert({ id: \"2\", text: \"Walk dog\" })\n  todosCollection.utils.writeUpdate({ id: \"3\", completed: true })\n  todosCollection.utils.writeDelete(\"4\")\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Await "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeBatch(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" to wait for the whole sync commit. The callback must be synchronous; the promises returned by writes inside it settle with the batch."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"real-world-example-websocket-integration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Real-World Example: WebSocket Integration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This example assumes the collection stays loaded for the app's lifetime, so the\nlistener can live at module scope."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Apply real-time changes from a WebSocket as direct writes\nws.on(\"todos:update\", (changes) => {\n  todosCollection.utils.writeBatch(() => {\n    changes.forEach((change) => {\n      switch (change.type) {\n        case \"insert\":\n          todosCollection.utils.writeInsert(change.data)\n          break\n        case \"update\":\n          todosCollection.utils.writeUpdate(change.data)\n          break\n        case \"delete\":\n          todosCollection.utils.writeDelete(change.id)\n          break\n      }\n    })\n  })\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In eager mode, these writes patch the cached query result without a\nrefetch. In on-demand mode, each direct write refetches the active queries.\nSee "},{"type":"link","href":"#direct-writes-and-query-sync","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Direct Writes and Query Sync"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A direct write is validated against the synced store after every earlier\nsync commit is accepted. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeUpdate"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeDelete"},{"type":"text","value":" reject their\npromise if the row is not in the synced store, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeInsert"},{"type":"text","value":" rejects if\nthe row is already there. They do not throw. An event can arrive before the\nfirst load finishes, or for a row the collection has not loaded. Handle the\nrejection, or use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeUpsert"},{"type":"text","value":" when events carry the full row."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In a persisted collection, a direct write can wait for an earlier sync\ncommit's durable write, so its rows can appear after the call returns. Await\nthe returned promise before you read them."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A direct write returns a promise. Await it to know that the write applied, and\ncatch its rejection to handle a validation error:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"try {\n  await todosCollection.utils.writeUpdate({ id: change.id, done: true })\n} catch (error) {\n  if (error instanceof UpdateOperationItemNotFoundError) {\n    // The row is not in the synced store yet. Refetch, or skip this event.\n    await todosCollection.utils.refetch()\n  } else {\n    throw error\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UpdateOperationItemNotFoundError"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DeleteOperationItemNotFoundError"},{"type":"text","value":"\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DuplicateKeyInBatchError"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/query-db-collection"},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DuplicateKeySyncError"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/db"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"example-incremental-updates","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Incremental Updates"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When the server returns computed fields (like server-generated IDs or timestamps), you can use direct writes to sync the server response without triggering a full refetch:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: fetchTodos,\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n      const newItems = transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.modified)\n\n      // Send to server and get back items with server-computed fields\n      const serverItems = await api.createTodos(newItems)\n\n      // Sync server-computed fields (like server-generated IDs, timestamps, etc.)\n      // to the collection's synced data store using direct writes\n      await collection.utils.writeBatch(() => {\n        serverItems.forEach((serverItem) => {\n          collection.utils.writeInsert(serverItem)\n        })\n      })\n\n      // No need to refetch - we've already synced the server response via direct writes\n      // (optimistic state is automatically replaced when handler completes)\n      return { refetch: false } // Transitional pre-1.0 opt-out\n    },\n\n    onUpdate: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n      const updates = transaction.mutations.map((m) => ({\n        id: m.key,\n        changes: m.changes,\n      }))\n      const serverItems = await api.updateTodos(updates)\n\n      // Sync server-computed fields from the update response\n      await collection.utils.writeBatch(() => {\n        serverItems.forEach((serverItem) => {\n          collection.utils.writeUpdate(serverItem)\n        })\n      })\n\n      // No refetch needed since we used direct writes\n      return { refetch: false } // Transitional pre-1.0 opt-out\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n// Usage is just like a regular collection\ntodosCollection.insert({ text: \"Buy milk\", completed: false })","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"server-pagination-with-live-queries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server pagination with live queries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveInfiniteQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" in React, Vue, and Svelte grows a local ordered query\nwindow. It does not run TanStack Query's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InfiniteQueryObserver"},{"type":"text","value":". Query\nCollections use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryObserver"},{"type":"text","value":", so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" receives\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta.loadSubsetOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", not "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageParam"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The previously ignored "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getNextPageParam"},{"type":"text","value":" option has been removed. Delete it\nfrom your hook config; passing it at runtime now throws a clear error.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialPageParam"},{"type":"text","value":" labels result pages only. It does not set a remote offset\nor server cursor."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For server loading, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncMode: 'on-demand'"},{"type":"text","value":" and make "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" fulfill the\nrequested filter, order, offset, and limit. Use a deterministic total order\n(for example, a timestamp followed by a unique ID). The loader may request a\nprefix, a suffix, a tie group, or the full filtered source. A request is not\nnecessarily one UI page: the hook fetches an extra row to determine\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hasNextPage"},{"type":"text","value":". Returning one capped endpoint page can incorrectly make the\nquery appear exhausted even when the server has more rows."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"endpoints-with-fixed-size-pages","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Endpoints with fixed-size pages"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your endpoint uses page numbers, drain enough server pages to fulfill each\nrequest. This example assumes a zero-based page API with a fixed size of 50.\nThe endpoint must apply the supplied filters and sorts "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"before"}]},{"type":"text","value":" pagination,\nkeep a consistent ordered result while its pages are read, and return\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nextPage: null"},{"type":"text","value":" only when it has authoritatively exhausted that result.\nThis example uses offset-based pagination. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"api.listPosts"},{"type":"text","value":" translates the full\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"where"},{"type":"text","value":" expression and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"orderBy"},{"type":"text","value":" options into the endpoint's syntax, and rejects\nunsupported expressions. The separate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cursor"},{"type":"text","value":" hints are deliberately unused;\ncursor-based adapters must handle those hints alongside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"where"},{"type":"text","value":", not treat them\nas already included in it. See "},{"type":"link","href":"#queryfn-and-predicate-push-down","children":[{"type":"text","value":"QueryFn and Predicate Push-Down"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor translation helpers. Do not drop predicates or filter after paginating:\neither changes the requested window."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createCollection } from '@tanstack/db'\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from '@tanstack/query-db-collection'\n\ntype Post = { id: number; createdAt: number; title: string }\nconst serverPageSize = 50\n\nconst postsCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: ['posts'],\n    queryClient,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    getKey: (post: Post) => post.id,\n    queryFn: async (ctx): Promise<Array<Post>> => {\n      const { where, orderBy, offset = 0, limit } = ctx.meta?.loadSubsetOptions ?? {}\n      const skip = offset % serverPageSize\n      let page: number | null = Math.floor(offset / serverPageSize)\n      const gathered: Array<Post> = []\n\n      while (page !== null && (limit === undefined || gathered.length < skip + limit)) {\n        ctx.signal.throwIfAborted()\n        const response: { rows: Array<Post>; nextPage: number | null } =\n          await api.listPosts({\n            page,\n            pageSize: serverPageSize,\n            where,\n            orderBy,\n            signal: ctx.signal,\n          })\n        gathered.push(...response.rows)\n        page = response.nextPage\n      }\n\n      return gathered.slice(skip, limit === undefined ? undefined : skip + limit)\n    },\n  }),\n)\n\n// React example; the collection protocol is the same for Vue and Svelte.\nconst { data, fetchNextPage, hasNextPage } = useLiveInfiniteQuery(\n  (q) => q.from({ post: postsCollection })\n    .orderBy(({ post }) => post.createdAt)\n    .orderBy(({ post }) => post.id),\n  { pageSize: 20 },\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reject failed requests instead of returning partial rows as success. An\nunlimited request must drain until the endpoint reports exhaustion. For an\nendpoint with opaque cursors, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCursorPager"},{"type":"text","value":" as shown below. The hook\ndoes not maintain remote cursor history on its own."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manually appending rows with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeUpsert"},{"type":"text","value":" is a separate, lower-level loading\nstrategy. It does not make an eager "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" incremental: a later successful\nrefetch still replaces its complete state and can remove appended rows.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"staleTime: Infinity"},{"type":"text","value":" does not prevent explicit refetch or invalidation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"endpoints-with-opaque-cursors","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Endpoints with opaque cursors"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query Collections ask for arbitrary windows, such as rows 40–59. An\nopaque-cursor API cannot jump to row 40. It can only fetch the first page, then\nfollow the continuation token returned with each page."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCursorPager"},{"type":"text","value":" bridges those two protocols. It stores complete backend\npages in your existing QueryClient and follows their cursors until it has enough\nrows for the requested "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"offset"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"limit"},{"type":"text","value":". A later request can reuse that\nfresh run of pages and fetch only the missing suffix. The hook still requests\nan extra row to decide "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hasNextPage"},{"type":"text","value":"; the pager just fulfills the resulting\nwindow."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createCollection } from '@tanstack/db'\nimport { createCursorPager, queryCollectionOptions } from '@tanstack/query-db-collection'\n\ntype Post = { id: number; createdAt: number; title: string }\n\nconst postsCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryClient,\n    queryKey: ['posts', 'rows'],\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    getKey: (post: Post) => post.id,\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      const { where, orderBy, offset = 0, limit } = ctx.meta?.loadSubsetOptions ?? {}\n\n      // Translate every filter and sort, or reject unsupported expressions.\n      // This stable, serializable value identifies one ordered result sequence.\n      const request = api.translatePostQuery({ where, orderBy })\n\n      const pager = createCursorPager<Post>({\n        queryClient,\n        queryKey: ['posts', 'cursor-pages', request],\n        staleTime: 60_000,\n        gcTime: 5 * 60_000,\n        fetchPage: async (cursor, signal) => {\n          const response = await api.listPosts({ ...request, cursor, signal })\n\n          // Only null means that the source is exhausted.\n          return { rows: response.items, nextCursor: response.nextCursor ?? null }\n        },\n      })\n\n      return pager.read({ offset, limit }, ctx.signal)\n    },\n  }),\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":5,"id":"give-each-result-sequence-its-own-key","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Give each result sequence its own key"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The pager's query key identifies one ordered backend result, not one requested\nwindow. Leave "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"offset"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"limit"},{"type":"text","value":" out of the key so wider reads can extend the\nsame cached prefix. Include everything that changes the result—such as the\ntenant, source, filters, and order."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep row data and cursor pages under sibling keys:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"['posts', 'rows']                         QueryCollection row array\n['posts', 'cursor-pages', request]        backend pages and cursors","lang":"text"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"They cannot share a key because the two cache entries have different shapes.\nDo not put cursor pages beneath the row key either: manual collection writes\ntarget entries under the row prefix. A shared resource prefix such as\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['posts']"},{"type":"text","value":" still lets you cancel or invalidate both kinds of data together."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The example creates a small pager object for each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" call. Those objects\nstill share cached pages and in-flight fetches through QueryClient. Each pager\nserializes its own reads. Retain one pager per result sequence only if calls\nmust also share that queue or you need to call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pager.reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" later."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":5,"id":"what-happens-when-the-collection-asks-for-rows","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What happens when the collection asks for rows"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For fresh data, the pager uses every cached page it can and fetches only the\nmissing suffix. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nextCursor: null"},{"type":"text","value":" is the sole exhaustion signal: a short or\nempty page with another cursor does not end the sequence. An omitted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"limit"},{"type":"text","value":"\ndrains the source; a zero limit performs no fetch. Repeated continuation tokens\nthrow instead of looping forever."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query owns freshness, garbage collection, retries, and invalidation. Pass\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"staleTime"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gcTime"},{"type":"text","value":" to the pager or let it inherit QueryClient defaults.\nConfigure retries through QueryClient defaults for the page-key prefix;\nimperative Query fetches do not retry by default. The pager keeps every page in\nits original form, so Query defaults for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxPages"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" do not apply."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When cached pages are stale, the next read refetches every page loaded so far,\nstarting with the first. It does this even for a shallow window because old\ncontinuation tokens belong to the old sequence. Staleness does not start a\nbackground timer. The page query has no lasting observer, so its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gcTime"},{"type":"text","value":" may\nexpire while the collection's published rows remain visible."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A failed read rejects rather than returning a partial window. Query keeps the\nlast successful pages, so a later read can retry. Treat their rows as immutable,\njust like other Query data. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"read()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns references from the cached pages,\nnot detached copies, and does not promise the same array or object identity\nacross calls."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":5,"id":"refresh-or-cancel-the-sequence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Refresh or cancel the sequence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After a mutation—or whenever you need a forced refresh—cancel the shared\nresource prefix before invalidating it:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"await queryClient.cancelQueries({ queryKey: ['posts'] })\nawait queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['posts'] })","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cancelling first stops an old page append from completing after invalidation and\nmarking the old sequence fresh again. It rejects readers waiting on that fetch\nwith an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortError"},{"type":"text","value":" and prevents a late result from entering the cache. The\nbackend must honor the signal if you also want to stop its network work."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Aborting the signal passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"read()"},{"type":"text","value":" rejects only that reader; it does not\ncancel a page fetch shared with another reader. A cancelling Query refetch moves\nwaiting readers to the replacement fetch. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection.utils.refetch()"},{"type":"text","value":" refreshes\nthe row queries but may reuse fresh cursor pages. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pager.reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" removes one\npager's exact page key and cancels its queued reads, but does not refresh the\ncollection's row query."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":5,"id":"backend-contract","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Backend contract"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The helper handles cursor traversal only. Your adapter must:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"translate every requested "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"where"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"orderBy"},{"type":"text","value":", or reject unsupported\nexpressions;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"apply filters and ordering before pagination;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"use a deterministic total order, including a unique tie-breaker;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"keep that ordered result consistent while its cursor sequence is read; and"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nextCursor: null"},{"type":"text","value":" only when the result is authoritatively exhausted."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"LoadSubsetOptions.cursor"},{"type":"text","value":" contains query-expression hints, not the backend's\nopaque token. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCursorPager"},{"type":"text","value":" therefore addresses the cursor sequence by\noffset and deliberately does not consume those hints. No client-side pager can\nmake a stable snapshot from an endpoint whose ordering changes between pages;\nthe endpoint must define its own consistency and cursor-expiry rules."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"important-behaviors","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Important Behaviors"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"full-state-sync","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full State Sync"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The query collection treats the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" result as the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"complete state"}]},{"type":"text","value":" of the collection. This means:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Items present in the collection but not in the query result will be deleted"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Items in the query result but not in the collection will be inserted"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Items present in both will be updated if they differ"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is important when the same entity type can be loaded from multiple REST\nendpoints. For example, do not point the same Query Collection at\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/api/documents/preview"},{"type":"text","value":" for one load and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/api/documents/deleted"},{"type":"text","value":" for another\nunless each result represents the complete state for that collection\nscope. A narrower endpoint can otherwise remove rows that were loaded from a\ndifferent endpoint."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For multiple endpoint or subset-loading use cases, choose the pattern that\nmatches your API semantics:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncMode: 'on-demand'"},{"type":"text","value":" when one logical collection can serve different\nsubsets of data. In this mode, query predicates ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"where"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"orderBy"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"limit"},{"type":"text","value":",\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"offset"},{"type":"text","value":") are passed to your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions"},{"type":"text","value":",\nletting you translate them into API parameters."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use separate Query Collections when endpoints represent distinct server scopes\nwhose results should not replace each other. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unionAll"},{"type":"text","value":" to combine them\ninto a single query when you need a unified view across endpoints."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use direct writes such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeUpsert"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeBatch"},{"type":"text","value":" for lower-level\nincremental loading when you intentionally want to merge server responses into\nthe synced store yourself."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"empty-array-behavior","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Empty Array Behavior"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" returns an empty array, "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"all items in the collection will be deleted"}]},{"type":"text","value":". This is because the collection interprets an empty array as \"the server has no items\"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// This will delete all items in the collection\nqueryFn: async () => []","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"handling-partial-incremental-fetches","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handling Partial/Incremental Fetches"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Since the query collection expects "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" to return the complete state, you can handle partial fetches by merging new data with existing data:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async ({ queryKey }) => {\n      // Get existing data from cache\n      const existingData = queryClient.getQueryData(queryKey) || []\n\n      // Fetch only new/updated items (e.g., changes since last sync)\n      const lastSyncTime = localStorage.getItem(\"todos-last-sync\")\n      const newData = await fetch(`/api/todos?since=${lastSyncTime}`).then(\n        (r) => r.json()\n      )\n\n      // Merge new data with existing data\n      const existingMap = new Map(existingData.map((item) => [item.id, item]))\n\n      // Apply updates and additions\n      newData.forEach((item) => {\n        existingMap.set(item.id, item)\n      })\n\n      // Handle deletions if your API provides them\n      if (newData.deletions) {\n        newData.deletions.forEach((id) => existingMap.delete(id))\n      }\n\n      // Update sync time\n      localStorage.setItem(\"todos-last-sync\", new Date().toISOString())\n\n      // Return the complete merged state\n      return Array.from(existingMap.values())\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  })\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This pattern allows you to:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fetch only incremental changes from your API"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Merge those changes with existing data"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Return the complete state that the collection expects"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Avoid the performance overhead of fetching all data every time"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"direct-writes-and-query-sync","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Direct Writes and Query Sync"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Direct writes update the collection immediately, or when a running mutation handler settles. In eager mode, they also patch the full-result TanStack Query cache in place."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In on-demand mode, each Query cache entry may represent a different predicate, order, limit, or offset. A full collection snapshot cannot safely replace those scoped results. Direct writes therefore refetch active enabled queries and remove inactive or disabled entries. A successful "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" result remains authoritative and may reconcile or replace a direct write."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To handle this properly:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ refetch: false }"},{"type":"text","value":" in persistence handlers to avoid the handler's additional refetch after a direct write. On-demand cache revalidation still runs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Make sure an on-demand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" returns the current server result for its pushed-down predicate, order, limit, and offset."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use eager mode when direct writes must update one complete cached result without a network request."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"complete-direct-write-api-reference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete Direct Write API Reference"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All direct write methods are available on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection.utils"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeInsert(data)"},{"type":"text","value":": Insert one or more items directly"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeUpdate(data)"},{"type":"text","value":": Update one or more items directly"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeDelete(keys)"},{"type":"text","value":": Delete one or more items directly"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeUpsert(data)"},{"type":"text","value":": Insert or update one or more items directly"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writeBatch(callback)"},{"type":"text","value":": Perform multiple operations atomically"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetch(opts?)"},{"type":"text","value":": Trigger a refetch of the query"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"queryfn-and-predicate-push-down","children":[{"type":"text","value":"QueryFn and Predicate Push-Down"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncMode: 'on-demand'"},{"type":"text","value":", the collection automatically pushes down query predicates (where clauses, orderBy, limit, and offset) to your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":". This allows you to fetch only the data needed for each specific query, rather than fetching the entire dataset."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"how-loadsubsetoptions-are-passed","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How LoadSubsetOptions Are Passed"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"LoadSubsetOptions are passed to your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" via the query context's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" property:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n  // Extract LoadSubsetOptions from the context\n  const { limit, offset, where, orderBy } = ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions\n\n  // Use these to fetch only the data you need\n  // - where: filter expression (AST)\n  // - orderBy: sort expression (AST)\n  // - limit: maximum number of rows\n  // - offset: number of rows to skip (for pagination)\n  // ...\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"where"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"orderBy"},{"type":"text","value":" fields are expression trees (AST - Abstract Syntax Tree) that need to be parsed. TanStack DB provides helper functions to make this easy."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"expression-helpers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Expression Helpers"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  parseWhereExpression,\n  parseOrderByExpression,\n  extractSimpleComparisons,\n  parseLoadSubsetOptions,\n} from '@tanstack/db'\n// Or from '@tanstack/query-db-collection' (re-exported for convenience)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These helpers allow you to parse expression trees without manually traversing complex AST structures."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"quick-start-simple-rest-api","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quick Start: Simple REST API"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createCollection } from '@tanstack/react-db'\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from '@tanstack/query-db-collection'\nimport { parseLoadSubsetOptions } from '@tanstack/db'\nimport { QueryClient } from '@tanstack/query-core'\n\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient()\n\nconst productsCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    id: 'products',\n    queryKey: ['products'],\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand', // Enable predicate push-down\n\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      const { limit, offset, where, orderBy } = ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions\n\n      // Parse the expressions into simple format\n      const parsed = parseLoadSubsetOptions({ where, orderBy, limit })\n\n      // Build query parameters from parsed filters\n      const params = new URLSearchParams()\n\n      // Add filters\n      parsed.filters.forEach(({ field, operator, value }) => {\n        const fieldName = field.join('.')\n        if (operator === 'eq') {\n          params.set(fieldName, String(value))\n        } else if (operator === 'lt') {\n          params.set(`${fieldName}_lt`, String(value))\n        } else if (operator === 'gt') {\n          params.set(`${fieldName}_gt`, String(value))\n        }\n      })\n\n      // Add sorting\n      if (parsed.sorts.length > 0) {\n        const sortParam = parsed.sorts\n          .map(s => `${s.field.join('.')}:${s.direction}`)\n          .join(',')\n        params.set('sort', sortParam)\n      }\n\n      // Add limit\n      if (parsed.limit) {\n        params.set('limit', String(parsed.limit))\n      }\n\n      // Add offset for pagination\n      if (offset) {\n        params.set('offset', String(offset))\n      }\n\n      const response = await fetch(`/api/products?${params}`)\n      return response.json()\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n// Usage with live queries\nimport { createLiveQueryCollection } from '@tanstack/react-db'\nimport { eq, lt, and } from '@tanstack/db'\n\nconst affordableElectronics = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q.from({ product: productsCollection })\n     .where(({ product }) => and(\n       eq(product.category, 'electronics'),\n       lt(product.price, 100)\n     ))\n     .orderBy(({ product }) => product.price, 'asc')\n     .limit(10)\n     .select(({ product }) => product)\n})\n\n// This triggers a queryFn call with:\n// GET /api/products?category=electronics&price_lt=100&sort=price:asc&limit=10\n// When paginating, offset is included: &offset=20","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"custom-handlers-for-complex-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Handlers for Complex APIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For APIs with specific formats, use custom handlers:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n  const { where, orderBy, limit } = ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions\n\n  // Use custom handlers to match your API's format\n  const filters = parseWhereExpression(where, {\n    handlers: {\n      eq: (field, value) => ({\n        field: field.join('.'),\n        op: 'equals',\n        value\n      }),\n      lt: (field, value) => ({\n        field: field.join('.'),\n        op: 'lessThan',\n        value\n      }),\n      and: (...conditions) => ({\n        operator: 'AND',\n        conditions\n      }),\n      or: (...conditions) => ({\n        operator: 'OR',\n        conditions\n      }),\n    }\n  })\n\n  const sorts = parseOrderByExpression(orderBy)\n\n  return api.query({\n    filters,\n    sort: sorts.map(s => ({\n      field: s.field.join('.'),\n      order: s.direction.toUpperCase()\n    })),\n    limit\n  })\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"graphql-example","children":[{"type":"text","value":"GraphQL Example"}]},{"type":"code","value":"queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n  const { where, orderBy, limit } = ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions\n\n  // Convert to a GraphQL where clause format\n  const whereClause = parseWhereExpression(where, {\n    handlers: {\n      eq: (field, value) => ({\n        [field.join('_')]: { _eq: value }\n      }),\n      lt: (field, value) => ({\n        [field.join('_')]: { _lt: value }\n      }),\n      and: (...conditions) => ({ _and: conditions }),\n      or: (...conditions) => ({ _or: conditions }),\n    }\n  })\n\n  // Convert to a GraphQL order_by format\n  const sorts = parseOrderByExpression(orderBy)\n  const orderByClause = sorts.map(s => ({\n    [s.field.join('_')]: s.direction\n  }))\n\n  const { data } = await graphqlClient.query({\n    query: gql`\n      query GetProducts($where: product_bool_exp, $orderBy: [product_order_by!], $limit: Int) {\n        product(where: $where, order_by: $orderBy, limit: $limit) {\n          id\n          name\n          category\n          price\n        }\n      }\n    `,\n    variables: {\n      where: whereClause,\n      orderBy: orderByClause,\n      limit\n    }\n  })\n\n  return data.product\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"expression-helper-api-reference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Expression Helper API Reference"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"parseloadsubsetoptions-options","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parseLoadSubsetOptions(options)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Convenience function that parses all LoadSubsetOptions at once. Good for simple use cases."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { filters, sorts, limit, offset } = parseLoadSubsetOptions(ctx.meta?.loadSubsetOptions)\n// filters: [{ field: ['category'], operator: 'eq', value: 'electronics' }]\n// sorts: [{ field: ['price'], direction: 'asc', nulls: 'last' }]\n// limit: 10\n// offset: 20 (for pagination)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"parsewhereexpression-expr-options","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parseWhereExpression(expr, options)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Parses a WHERE expression using custom handlers for each operator. Use this for complete control over the output format."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const filters = parseWhereExpression(where, {\n  handlers: {\n    eq: (field, value) => ({ [field.join('.')]: value }),\n    lt: (field, value) => ({ [`${field.join('.')}_lt`]: value }),\n    and: (...filters) => Object.assign({}, ...filters)\n  },\n  onUnknownOperator: (operator, args) => {\n    console.warn(`Unsupported operator: ${operator}`)\n    return null\n  }\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"parseorderbyexpression-orderby","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parseOrderByExpression(orderBy)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Parses an ORDER BY expression into a simple array."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const sorts = parseOrderByExpression(orderBy)\n// Returns: [{ field: ['price'], direction: 'asc', nulls: 'last' }]","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"extractsimplecomparisons-expr","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extractSimpleComparisons(expr)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Extracts simple AND-ed comparisons from a WHERE expression. Note: Only works for simple AND conditions."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const comparisons = extractSimpleComparisons(where)\n// Returns: [\n//   { field: ['category'], operator: 'eq', value: 'electronics' },\n//   { field: ['price'], operator: 'lt', value: 100 }\n// ]","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"supported-operators","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Supported Operators"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq"},{"type":"text","value":" - Equality (=)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gt"},{"type":"text","value":" - Greater than (>)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gte"},{"type":"text","value":" - Greater than or equal (>=)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lt"},{"type":"text","value":" - Less than (<)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lte"},{"type":"text","value":" - Less than or equal (<=)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"and"},{"type":"text","value":" - Logical AND"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"or"},{"type":"text","value":" - Logical OR"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"in"},{"type":"text","value":" - IN clause"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"using-query-key-builders","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using Query Key Builders"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncMode: 'on-demand'"},{"type":"text","value":", a static key such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryKey: ['products']"},{"type":"text","value":"\nautomatically includes the subset demand identity. Filters, ordering, limits,\noffsets, and cursor hints distinguish cache entries when they change the\nrequested data. Prefer a static key when you do not need a custom key structure."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A function-based "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryKey"},{"type":"text","value":" replaces that automatic key construction. Include\nall options that change the requested data; omitting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"offset"},{"type":"text","value":", for example,\ncan make different windows share a cache entry. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getLoadSubsetDemandKey"},{"type":"text","value":"\nfrom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/db"},{"type":"text","value":" to retain the same subset identity as a static key, including\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"limit: 0"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { getLoadSubsetDemandKey } from '@tanstack/db'\n\nconst productsCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    id: 'products',\n    queryKey: (opts) => {\n      const demandKey = getLoadSubsetDemandKey(opts)\n      return demandKey === undefined ? ['products'] : ['products', demandKey]\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => { /* ... */ },\n  })\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"query-key-prefix-convention","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query Key Prefix Convention"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When using a function-based "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryKey"},{"type":"text","value":", all derived keys "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"must extend the base key as a prefix"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The base key is what your function returns when called with no options ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryKey({})"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query uses prefix matching for cache operations internally. The query collection relies on this to find all cache entries belonging to a collection — including stale entries from destroyed query observers that are still held in cache due to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gcTime"},{"type":"text","value":". If derived keys don't share the base prefix, cache updates may silently miss entries, leading to stale data."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The example above returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['products']"},{"type":"text","value":" for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryKey({})"},{"type":"text","value":" and keeps it as\nthe prefix of every derived key. Avoid changing the prefix for subset demands:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// ❌ Wrong: base key ['products-all'] is NOT a prefix of ['products-filtered', ...]\nqueryKey: (opts) => {\n  const demandKey = getLoadSubsetDemandKey(opts)\n  return demandKey === undefined\n    ? ['products-all']\n    : ['products-filtered', demandKey]\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"tips","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tips"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parseLoadSubsetOptions"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for simple use cases"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use custom handlers"}]},{"type":"text","value":" via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parseWhereExpression"},{"type":"text","value":" for APIs with specific formats"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handle unsupported operators"}]},{"type":"text","value":" with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUnknownOperator"},{"type":"text","value":" callback"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Log parsed results"}]},{"type":"text","value":" during development to verify correctness"}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"overview","text":"Overview","level":2},{"id":"installation","text":"Installation","level":2},{"id":"basic-usage","text":"Basic Usage","level":2},{"id":"configuration-options","text":"Configuration Options","level":2},{"id":"required-options","text":"Required Options","level":3},{"id":"request-scoped-queryclient","text":"Request-scoped QueryClient","level":3},{"id":"business-scoped-collection-factories","text":"Business-Scoped Collection Factories","level":3},{"id":"query-options","text":"Query Options","level":3},{"id":"request-cancellation-with-queryfunctioncontext-signal","text":"Request Cancellation with QueryFunctionContext.signal","level":3},{"id":"using-with-queryoptions","text":"Using with queryOptions(...)","level":3},{"id":"initial-data","text":"Initial Data","level":3},{"id":"selecting-rows-from-wrapped-responses","text":"Selecting Rows from Wrapped Responses","level":3},{"id":"collection-options","text":"Collection Options","level":3},{"id":"persistence-handlers","text":"Persistence Handlers","level":3},{"id":"extending-meta-with-custom-properties","text":"Extending Meta with Custom Properties","level":2},{"id":"type-safe-meta-access","text":"Type-Safe Meta Access","level":3},{"id":"adding-custom-meta-properties","text":"Adding Custom Meta Properties","level":3},{"id":"important-notes","text":"Important Notes","level":3},{"id":"example-api-request-context","text":"Example: API Request Context","level":3},{"id":"persistence-handlers-2","text":"Persistence Handlers","level":2},{"id":"controlling-refetch-behavior","text":"Controlling Refetch Behavior","level":3},{"id":"current-behavior-pre-1-0","text":"Current Behavior (Pre-1.0)","level":4},{"id":"v1-0-behavior-future","text":"v1.0 Behavior (Future)","level":4},{"id":"when-to-skip-refetch","text":"When to Skip Refetch","level":4},{"id":"utility-methods","text":"Utility Methods","level":2},{"id":"direct-writes","text":"Direct Writes","level":2},{"id":"understanding-the-data-stores","text":"Understanding the Data Stores","level":3},{"id":"when-to-use-direct-writes","text":"When to Use Direct Writes","level":3},{"id":"individual-write-operations","text":"Individual Write Operations","level":3},{"id":"batch-operations","text":"Batch Operations","level":3},{"id":"real-world-example-websocket-integration","text":"Real-World Example: WebSocket Integration","level":3},{"id":"example-incremental-updates","text":"Example: Incremental Updates","level":3},{"id":"server-pagination-with-live-queries","text":"Server pagination with live queries","level":3},{"id":"endpoints-with-fixed-size-pages","text":"Endpoints with fixed-size pages","level":4},{"id":"endpoints-with-opaque-cursors","text":"Endpoints with opaque cursors","level":4},{"id":"give-each-result-sequence-its-own-key","text":"Give each result sequence its own key","level":5},{"id":"what-happens-when-the-collection-asks-for-rows","text":"What happens when the collection asks for rows","level":5},{"id":"refresh-or-cancel-the-sequence","text":"Refresh or cancel the sequence","level":5},{"id":"backend-contract","text":"Backend contract","level":5},{"id":"important-behaviors","text":"Important Behaviors","level":2},{"id":"full-state-sync","text":"Full State Sync","level":3},{"id":"empty-array-behavior","text":"Empty Array Behavior","level":3},{"id":"handling-partial-incremental-fetches","text":"Handling Partial/Incremental Fetches","level":3},{"id":"direct-writes-and-query-sync","text":"Direct Writes and Query Sync","level":3},{"id":"complete-direct-write-api-reference","text":"Complete Direct Write API Reference","level":2},{"id":"queryfn-and-predicate-push-down","text":"QueryFn and Predicate Push-Down","level":2},{"id":"how-loadsubsetoptions-are-passed","text":"How LoadSubsetOptions Are Passed","level":3},{"id":"expression-helpers","text":"Expression Helpers","level":3},{"id":"quick-start-simple-rest-api","text":"Quick Start: Simple REST API","level":3},{"id":"custom-handlers-for-complex-apis","text":"Custom Handlers for Complex APIs","level":3},{"id":"graphql-example","text":"GraphQL Example","level":3},{"id":"expression-helper-api-reference","text":"Expression Helper API Reference","level":3},{"id":"parseloadsubsetoptions-options","text":"parseLoadSubsetOptions(options)","level":4},{"id":"parsewhereexpression-expr-options","text":"parseWhereExpression(expr, options)","level":4},{"id":"parseorderbyexpression-orderby","text":"parseOrderByExpression(orderBy)","level":4},{"id":"extractsimplecomparisons-expr","text":"extractSimpleComparisons(expr)","level":4},{"id":"supported-operators","text":"Supported Operators","level":3},{"id":"using-query-key-builders","text":"Using Query Key Builders","level":3},{"id":"query-key-prefix-convention","text":"Query Key Prefix Convention","level":4},{"id":"tips","text":"Tips","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nQuery collections provide seamless integration between TanStack DB and TanStack Query, enabling automatic synchronization between your local database and remote data sources.\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `@tanstack/query-db-collection` package allows you to create collections that:\n\n- Automatically fetch remote data via TanStack Query\n- Support optimistic updates with automatic rollback on errors\n- Handle persistence through customizable mutation handlers\n- Provide direct write capabilities for directly writing to the sync store\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\nnpm install @tanstack/query-db-collection @tanstack/query-core @tanstack/db\n```\n\n## Basic Usage\n\n```typescript\nimport { QueryClient } from \"@tanstack/query-core\"\nimport { DbClient, collectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/db\"\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\"\n\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient()\nconst db = new DbClient({ queryClient })\n\nconst todosCollection = collectionOptions(\"todos\", (client) =>\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    id: \"todos\",\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async () => {\n      const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\")\n      return response.json()\n    },\n    queryClient: client.requireDependency<QueryClient>(\"queryClient\"),\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  })\n)\n\nconst todos = db.collection(todosCollection)\n```\n\n## Configuration Options\n\nThe `queryCollectionOptions` function accepts the following options:\n\n### Required Options\n\n- `queryKey`: The query key for TanStack Query. Can be a static array or a function that receives `LoadSubsetOptions` and returns a key. When using a function, all returned keys must share the base key (`queryKey({})`) as a prefix — see [Query Key Prefix Convention](#query-key-prefix-convention).\n- `queryFn`: Function that fetches data from the server\n- `queryClient`: TanStack Query client instance\n- `getKey`: Function to extract the unique key from an item\n\n### Request-scoped QueryClient\n\n`queryCollectionOptions` needs a `queryClient`. In SSR, TanStack Start, tests,\nor multi-tenant apps, that client is request-local rather than module-global.\nPut it on `DbClient`, then resolve it inside the collection descriptor factory:\n\n```typescript\nimport { QueryClient } from \"@tanstack/query-core\"\nimport { DbClient, collectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/db\"\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\"\n\ninterface Todo {\n  id: string\n  title: string\n}\n\nexport const todoCollection = collectionOptions(\"todos\", (client) =>\n  queryCollectionOptions<Todo>({\n    id: \"todos\",\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async () => {\n      const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\")\n      return response.json() as Promise<Array<Todo>>\n    },\n    queryClient: client.requireDependency<QueryClient>(\"queryClient\"),\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n  })\n)\n\nexport function createRequestClients() {\n  const queryClient = new QueryClient()\n  const dbClient = new DbClient({ queryClient })\n  return { queryClient, dbClient }\n}\n```\n\n`dbClient.collection(todoCollection)` memoizes one collection instance for that\ndescriptor and client. A second `DbClient` materializes fresh adapter state and\nuses its own `QueryClient`.\n\nPassing `queryClient` directly to `queryCollectionOptions` remains supported for\n`createCollection(...)` and existing apps. When a descriptor is materialized,\nan explicit `DbClient` dependency takes precedence; the configured\n`queryClient` is the backwards-compatible fallback.\n\n### Business-Scoped Collection Factories\n\nA tenant, project, account, or route parameter can define a **business scope**:\nthe server resource that a collection represents. Include the scope in the\ndescriptor id, Query key, and `queryFn`. This extends the\n[request-scoped QueryClient pattern](#request-scoped-queryclient) with an\nexplicit scope parameter:\n\n```typescript\ninterface Todo {\n  id: string\n  title: string\n  projectId: string\n}\n\nasync function fetchProjectTodos(projectId: string): Promise<Array<Todo>> {\n  const response = await fetch(`/api/projects/${projectId}/todos`)\n  return response.json()\n}\n\nfunction createProjectTodosDescriptor(\n  projectId: string,\n) {\n  return collectionOptions(`project:${projectId}:todos`, (client) =>\n    queryCollectionOptions<Todo>({\n      id: `project:${projectId}:todos`,\n      queryKey: [\"projects\", projectId, \"todos\"],\n      queryFn: () => fetchProjectTodos(projectId),\n      queryClient: client.requireDependency<QueryClient>(\"queryClient\"),\n      getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    })\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe scope is part of the descriptor identity. `DbClient` resolves separately\ncreated descriptors with the same id to the same collection, so a React hook\ncan create the descriptor from its current parameters:\n\n```typescript\nexport function useProjectTodos(projectId: string) {\n  return useDbClient().collection(createProjectTodosDescriptor(projectId))\n}\n```\n\nOnly the first descriptor for an id is materialized. Include every scope value\nthat changes the collection in both its descriptor id and Query key. Call\n`await dbClient.cleanup()` when the client scope ends.\n\nA business scope is separate from a **relational subset** requested by a live query. With `syncMode: \"on-demand\"`, `LoadSubsetOptions` describes predicates, ordering, limits, and offsets within one business-scoped collection. These options reach `queryFn` through `ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions` and determine the subset Query keys. See [QueryFn and Predicate Push-Down](#queryfn-and-predicate-push-down).\n\nDo not create a collection for each `where`, `orderBy`, or `limit`. Reuse the business-scoped collection and let on-demand loading represent those subsets. Create separate collections only for distinct server resources.\n\n### Query Options\n\nQuery Collections use TanStack Query internally and expose supported Query observer options as top-level `queryCollectionOptions` fields.\n\nThe following top-level Query Collection options are forwarded to the underlying Query observer:\n\n- `select`: Function that extracts the row array TanStack DB materializes from a wrapped Query response\n- `enabled`: Whether the query should automatically run (default: `true`)\n- `refetchInterval`: Refetch interval in milliseconds\n- `retry`: Retry configuration for failed queries\n- `retryDelay`: Delay between retries\n- `staleTime`: How long data is considered fresh\n- `gcTime`: How long unused query data stays in the Query cache\n- `refetchOnWindowFocus`: Whether to refetch when the window regains focus\n- `refetchOnReconnect`: Whether to refetch when the network reconnects\n- `refetchOnMount`: Whether to refetch when the observer mounts\n- `networkMode`: Query network mode\n- `initialData`: Initial Query response for eager collections\n- `initialDataUpdatedAt`: Timestamp used by TanStack Query to determine initial data freshness\n- `meta`: Metadata passed to the query function context. Query Collections may add `loadSubsetOptions` for on-demand queries.\n\n```ts\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: fetchTodos,\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    refetchOnWindowFocus: true,\n    refetchOnReconnect: true,\n    refetchOnMount: \"always\",\n    networkMode: \"online\",\n  })\n)\n```\n\nTop-level `meta` is always merged by Query Collection so it can add on-demand `loadSubsetOptions`. Other supported top-level Query options are only passed to TanStack Query when you define them. If you omit them, `QueryClient.defaultOptions` can still apply.\n\nSome fields are owned or reinterpreted by the collection adapter rather than treated as ordinary Query option pass-through:\n\n- `queryKey`: Identifies the Query cache entry and, in on-demand mode, may be built from load-subset options.\n- `queryFn`: Fetches the complete collection state or the requested on-demand subset.\n- `select`: Extracts array rows from wrapped responses before they are stored in the collection. This is not the same contract as TanStack Query's `select` option.\n- `queryClient`: Supplies the Query client instance used by the collection.\n- `syncMode`: Controls whether the collection syncs eagerly or on demand.\n- `getKey`: Extracts each row's stable TanStack DB key.\n- Mutation handlers such as `onInsert`, `onUpdate`, and `onDelete`.\n\nSome TanStack Query fields are owned or reinterpreted by Query Collection and are intentionally not exposed as ordinary Query observer options:\n\n- `queryKey`, `queryFn`, and `queryClient`\n- `select` (Query Collection uses this for row extraction, not TanStack Query's observer-level `select` contract)\n- `meta` (merged by Query Collection so on-demand `loadSubsetOptions` can be included)\n- `subscribed` (Query Collection owns the observer subscription lifecycle)\n- `structuralSharing` and `notifyOnChangeProps` (managed by Query Collection synchronization)\n\n`placeholderData` is intentionally unsupported. TanStack Query treats placeholder data as observer-local presentation state rather than cached Query data. Materializing it would expose temporary UI data as collection-wide normalized rows. Render placeholders in the consuming UI instead.\n\n### Request Cancellation with `QueryFunctionContext.signal`\n\nTanStack Query passes an `AbortSignal` to `queryFn` through the query function\ncontext. Forward `ctx.signal` to `fetch` or another abortable client to make the\nrequest cancellable:\n\n```typescript\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\", {\n        signal: ctx.signal,\n      })\n\n      if (!response.ok) {\n        throw new Error(\"Failed to fetch todos\")\n      }\n\n      return response.json() as Promise<Array<Todo>>\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n  }),\n)\n```\n\nExplicit collection cleanup cancels each exact Query key the collection is\ncurrently tracking before removing it from the Query cache:\n\n```typescript\nawait todosCollection.cleanup()\n```\n\nThe underlying request is aborted only when its client consumes `ctx.signal`.\nA client that ignores the signal may continue its request even though the\ncollection has been cleaned up.\n\nAn unloaded on-demand subset is no longer tracked. A later explicit collection\ncleanup does not revisit its Query key.\n\nQuery cache entries are shared within a `QueryClient`. Explicit cleanup can\naffect other consumers using the same exact Query keys.\n\nOn-demand subset unloading does not explicitly call\n`queryClient.cancelQueries()`. It removes the subset's Query observer. If this\nwas the final observer and the query function consumed `ctx.signal`, TanStack\nQuery aborts the request. If the signal was ignored, or another observer still\nuses the same exact Query key, the request may finish and remain cached until\n`gcTime`.\n\n### Using with `queryOptions(...)`\n\nIf your app already uses TanStack Query's `queryOptions` helper (e.g. from `@tanstack/react-query`), you can spread compatible top-level options into `queryCollectionOptions`. Note that `queryFn` must be explicitly provided since query collections require it both in types and at runtime, and Query Collection's `select` option is for row extraction rather than TanStack Query observer-level selection:\n\n```typescript\nimport { QueryClient } from \"@tanstack/query-core\"\nimport { DbClient, collectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/db\"\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\"\nimport { queryOptions } from \"@tanstack/react-query\"\n\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient()\nconst db = new DbClient({ queryClient })\n\nconst listOptions = queryOptions({\n  queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n  queryFn: async () => {\n    const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\")\n    return response.json() as Promise<Array<{ id: string; title: string }>>\n  },\n})\n\nconst todosCollection = collectionOptions(\"todos\", (client) =>\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    id: \"todos\",\n    ...listOptions,\n    queryFn: (context) => listOptions.queryFn!(context),\n    queryClient: client.requireDependency<QueryClient>(\"queryClient\"),\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  }),\n)\n\nconst todos = db.collection(todosCollection)\n```\n\nIf `queryFn` is missing at runtime, `queryCollectionOptions` throws `QueryFnRequiredError`.\n\n### Initial Data\n\nEager Query Collections support TanStack Query's `initialData` and\n`initialDataUpdatedAt` options. Initial data has the original Query response\nshape, is stored in the Query cache, and is immediately materialized as\nnormalized collection rows. TanStack Query uses `initialDataUpdatedAt` together\nwith `staleTime` to decide whether to fetch.\n\n```typescript\nconst serverRenderedAt = Date.now()\nconst initialTodos = [\n  { id: \"1\", title: \"Write documentation\" },\n  { id: \"2\", title: \"Ship initial data support\" },\n]\n\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: fetchTodos,\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    initialData: initialTodos,\n    initialDataUpdatedAt: serverRenderedAt,\n    staleTime: 60_000,\n  }),\n)\n```\n\nAn existing cached or hydrated Query response takes precedence over\n`initialData`. Query keys remain the cache identity: two collections using the\nsame QueryClient and exact Query key observe one shared Query document, and a\nlater collection's initializer does not replace it. Use distinct Query keys for\nindependent documents.\n\nInitial data is supported only for eager collections. A collection-wide value\ncannot establish row membership for arbitrary on-demand predicates, ordering,\nlimits, and offsets. For `syncMode: \"on-demand\"`, seed or hydrate the exact\nderived Query cache entries instead.\n\nIf a stale initial response triggers a fetch, the initial rows remain available\nwhile it is in flight. A successful response reconciles them through the normal\nrow ownership pipeline; an error retains the initial rows. Direct writes patch\nthe eager Query cache in place. On-demand direct writes revalidate scoped\nentries as described below.\n\n### Selecting Rows from Wrapped Responses\n\nMany APIs return rows inside a response envelope that also contains metadata such as pagination cursors, totals, or request information. Use `select` to extract the row array that TanStack DB should materialize:\n\n```typescript\ninterface TodosResponse {\n  items: Array<{ id: string; title: string }>\n  nextCursor?: string\n  total: number\n}\n\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async (): Promise<TodosResponse> => {\n      const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\")\n      return response.json()\n    },\n    initialData: {\n      items: [{ id: \"1\", title: \"Initial todo\" }],\n      nextCursor: undefined,\n      total: 1,\n    },\n    select: (response) => response.items,\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  }),\n)\n```\n\n`select` is a query-db-collection row extraction hook. It tells TanStack DB which rows to materialize while the TanStack Query cache keeps the original query response shape. In the example above, `queryClient.getQueryData([\"todos\"])` still returns the full `TodosResponse`, including `nextCursor` and `total`.\n\nThe same projection applies to `initialData`: provide the complete response\nenvelope, and Query Collection materializes the rows returned by `select` while\npreserving the envelope in the Query cache.\n\nThis differs from TanStack Query's observer-level `select`: query-db-collection uses this option to bridge Query's response object into DB's normalized row store.\n\nIn eager mode, direct write utilities such as `writeInsert`, `writeUpdate`, and `writeDelete` make a best-effort attempt to update the matching row array inside wrapped Query cache entries while preserving wrapper metadata. In on-demand mode, they revalidate active scoped queries and remove inactive or disabled cache entries instead of patching them with the full collection snapshot.\n\nThis works automatically for simple wrappers such as:\n\n- `{ data: [...] }`\n- `{ items: [...] }`\n- `{ results: [...] }`\n\nDerived projections, such as `select: (response) => response.edges.map((edge) => edge.node)`, are read-side row extraction only. query-db-collection cannot generally reconstruct the original response envelope from updated rows. Refetch or invalidate the query if the wrapped cache must exactly reflect direct writes for a derived projection.\n\n### Collection Options\n\n- `id`: Unique identifier for the collection\n- `schema`: Schema for validating items\n- `sync`: Custom sync configuration\n- `startSync`: Whether to start syncing immediately (default: `true`)\n\n### Persistence Handlers\n\n- `onInsert`: Handler called before insert operations\n- `onUpdate`: Handler called before update operations\n- `onDelete`: Handler called before delete operations\n\n## Extending Meta with Custom Properties\n\nThe `meta` option allows you to pass additional metadata to your query function. By default, Query Collections automatically include `loadSubsetOptions` in the meta object, which contains filtering, sorting, and pagination options for on-demand queries.\n\n`loadSubsetOptions` is request-scoped. Query functions can read it through\n`ctx.meta`, but it is excluded when Query metadata is serialized for SSR.\nHydrated Query metadata therefore does not contain the server request options;\nnew on-demand requests supply their own options. Other enumerable `meta`\nproperties retain TanStack Query's normal dehydration behavior.\n\nTreat `ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions` and its nested request data as read-only.\nDo not edit expression nodes, ordering options, Dates, byte arrays, or membership\narrays. Build separate API parameters instead. Core retains request data without\ncloning it; changing submitted data can make the request disagree with its cache\nkey. To change a query constant, supply a new value rather than mutating the old\none. Cancellation through the request's `AbortSignal` remains supported.\n\n### Type-Safe Meta Access\n\nThe `ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions` property is automatically typed as `LoadSubsetOptions` without requiring any additional imports or type assertions:\n\n```typescript\nimport { parseLoadSubsetOptions } from \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\"\n\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"products\"],\n    syncMode: \"on-demand\",\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      // ✅ Type-safe access - no @ts-ignore needed!\n      const options = parseLoadSubsetOptions(ctx.meta?.loadSubsetOptions)\n\n      // Use the parsed options to fetch only what you need\n      return api.getProducts(options)\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  })\n)\n```\n\n### Adding Custom Meta Properties\n\nYou can extend the meta type to include your own custom properties using TypeScript's module augmentation:\n\n```typescript\n// In a global type definition file (e.g., types.d.ts or global.d.ts)\ndeclare module \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\" {\n  interface QueryCollectionMeta {\n    // Add your custom properties here\n    userId?: string\n    includeDeleted?: boolean\n    cacheTTL?: number\n  }\n}\n```\n\nOnce you've extended the interface, your custom properties are fully typed throughout your application:\n\n```typescript\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      // ✅ Both loadSubsetOptions and custom properties are typed\n      const { loadSubsetOptions, userId, includeDeleted } = ctx.meta\n\n      return api.getTodos({\n        ...parseLoadSubsetOptions(loadSubsetOptions),\n        userId,\n        includeDeleted,\n      })\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    // Pass custom meta alongside Query Collection defaults\n    meta: {\n      userId: \"user-123\",\n      includeDeleted: false,\n    },\n  })\n)\n```\n\n### Important Notes\n\n- The module augmentation pattern follows TanStack Query's official approach for typing meta\n- `QueryCollectionMeta` is an interface (not a type alias), enabling proper TypeScript declaration merging\n- Your custom properties are merged with the base `loadSubsetOptions` property\n- All meta properties must be compatible with `Record<string, unknown>`\n- The augmentation should be done in a file that's included in your TypeScript compilation\n\n### Example: API Request Context\n\nA common use case is passing request context to your query function:\n\n```typescript\n// types.d.ts\ndeclare module \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\" {\n  interface QueryCollectionMeta {\n    authToken?: string\n    locale?: string\n    version?: string\n  }\n}\n\n// collections.ts\nconst productsCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"products\"],\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      const { loadSubsetOptions, authToken, locale, version } = ctx.meta\n\n      return api.getProducts({\n        ...parseLoadSubsetOptions(loadSubsetOptions),\n        headers: {\n          Authorization: `Bearer ${authToken}`,\n          \"Accept-Language\": locale,\n          \"API-Version\": version,\n        },\n      })\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    meta: {\n      authToken: session.token,\n      locale: \"en-US\",\n      version: \"v1\",\n    },\n  })\n)\n```\n\n## Persistence Handlers\n\nYou can define handlers that are called when mutations occur. These handlers persist changes to your backend:\n\n```typescript\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: fetchTodos,\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      const newItems = transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.modified)\n      await api.createTodos(newItems)\n      // Auto-refetch happens after handler completes (pre-1.0 behavior)\n    },\n\n    onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      const updates = transaction.mutations.map((m) => ({\n        id: m.key,\n        changes: m.changes,\n      }))\n      await api.updateTodos(updates)\n    },\n\n    onDelete: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      const ids = transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.key)\n      await api.deleteTodos(ids)\n    },\n  })\n)\n```\n\n> **Note**: QueryCollection currently auto-refetches after handlers complete. See [Controlling Refetch Behavior](#controlling-refetch-behavior) for details on this transitional behavior.\n\n### Controlling Refetch Behavior\n\n> **⚠️ Transitional API**: QueryCollection currently auto-refetches after handlers complete. This behavior is deprecated and will be removed in v1.0. See the migration notes below.\n\n#### Current Behavior (Pre-1.0)\n\nBy default, QueryCollection automatically refetches after each handler completes. To **skip** auto-refetch, return `{ refetch: false }`:\n\n```typescript\nonInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n  await api.createTodos(transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.modified))\n\n  // Skip auto-refetch - use this when server doesn't modify the data\n  return { refetch: false }\n}\n```\n\nIf you don't return `{ refetch: false }`, auto-refetch happens automatically.\n\n#### v1.0 Behavior (Future)\n\nIn v1.0, auto-refetch will be **removed**. Handlers will need to explicitly call `collection.utils.refetch()` when refetching is needed:\n\n```typescript\nonInsert: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n  await api.createTodos(transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.modified))\n\n  // Explicitly trigger refetch when you need server state\n  await collection.utils.refetch()\n}\n```\n\nWhen no user mutation is persisting and no mutation handler is active, the\n`refetch()` promise resolves after every accepted result from that call has been\napplied to Collection rows. This is also the application barrier when the\nresult has no row diff and therefore emits no change event. Rejection is\nfail-fast: with several tracked Queries, one failure may reject the call while\nindependent work remains pending.\n\nWhile a user mutation is persisting or a Collection mutation handler is active,\nnormal application is queued behind that transaction. To avoid a circular wait\nor an unrelated persistence delay, all overlapping `refetch()` calls resolve at\nthe Query fetch boundary. This rule applies whether the mutation or the refetch\nstarts first. It includes calls through the handler parameter, captured or\nexternal Collection references, manual transaction mutation functions, and\n`createOptimisticAction` mutation functions. Code can await the mutation's\n`isPersisted.promise` when it needs the complete transaction and its queued\nCollection application. Utilities that refetch, including `clearError()`,\ninherit the same phase boundary.\n\nThe handler's `collection` parameter and every matching\n`transaction.mutations[n].collection` alias preserve the external Collection\nidentity. Strict equality, `Map`, and `WeakMap` lookups therefore continue to\nwork in shared persistence handlers. Phase tracking changes the refetch wait\nboundary without replacing the Collection, transaction, or mutation objects.\n\nAt the application boundary, `throwOnError` applies to both Query fetch errors\nand errors applying an accepted result to Collection rows. With\n`throwOnError: true`, application failure or cancellation rejects the call;\ncancellation uses an `AbortError`. With `throwOnError: false` (the default),\n`refetch()` returns its Query results instead. At the mutation-phase fetch\nboundary, `throwOnError` applies only to the Query fetch because application\nhappens after that call settles. A later application failure is recorded by the\nCollection's error utilities.\n\nTo skip refetch in v1.0, simply don't call `refetch()`:\n\n```typescript\nonInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n  await api.createTodos(transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.modified))\n\n  // No refetch call = no refetch (v1.0 behavior)\n}\n```\n\n#### When to Skip Refetch\n\nSkip refetching when:\n\n- You're confident the server state exactly matches what you sent (no server-side processing)\n- You're handling state updates through other mechanisms (like WebSockets or direct writes)\n- You want to optimize for fewer network requests\n\n## Utility Methods\n\nThe collection provides these utility methods via `collection.utils`:\n\n- `refetch(opts?)`: Refetch every tracked Query and await the applicable fetch or application boundary\n  - `opts.throwOnError`: Whether Query fetch or Collection application errors reject an application-boundary call (default: `false`); mutation-phase calls cover fetch errors only\n  - Bypasses `enabled: false` to support imperative/manual refetching patterns (similar to hook `refetch()` behavior)\n  - Returns the tracked Queries' `QueryObserverResult` values in tracked-key order\n  - Preserves an `undefined` slot if a tracked Query is removed while the refetch starts\n- `clearError()`: Refetch with errors enabled; retain the recorded error until a successful result applies to the Collection\n  - Uses the same phase-dependent boundary as `refetch()`. During a mutation handler, the promise can resolve at the fetch boundary before the result applies and clears the error.\n\n## Direct Writes\n\nDirect writes are intended for scenarios where the normal query/mutation flow doesn't fit your needs. They write directly to the synced data store and bypass the optimistic update system. Their Query cache behavior depends on the collection's sync mode.\n\n### Understanding the Data Stores\n\nQuery Collections maintain two data stores:\n\n1. **Synced Data Store** - The authoritative state synchronized with the server via `queryFn`\n2. **Optimistic Mutations Store** - Temporary changes that are applied optimistically before server confirmation\n\nNormal collection operations (insert, update, delete) create optimistic mutations that are:\n\n- Applied immediately to the UI\n- Sent to the server via persistence handlers\n- Rolled back automatically if the server request fails\n- Replaced with server data when the query refetches\n\nDirect writes bypass this system entirely and write directly to the synced data store, making them useful for handling real-time updates from alternative sources. Active on-demand queries still refetch so each scoped cache remains authoritative for its own request.\n\n### When to Use Direct Writes\n\nDirect writes should be used when:\n\n- You need to sync real-time updates from WebSockets or server-sent events\n- You're dealing with large datasets where refetching everything is too expensive\n- You receive incremental updates or server-computed field updates\n- You need to implement complex pagination or partial data loading scenarios\n\n### Individual Write Operations\n\n```typescript\n// Insert a new item directly to the synced data store\ntodosCollection.utils.writeInsert({\n  id: \"1\",\n  text: \"Buy milk\",\n  completed: false,\n})\n\n// Update an existing item in the synced data store\ntodosCollection.utils.writeUpdate({ id: \"1\", completed: true })\n\n// Delete an item from the synced data store\ntodosCollection.utils.writeDelete(\"1\")\n\n// Upsert (insert or update) in the synced data store\ntodosCollection.utils.writeUpsert({\n  id: \"1\",\n  text: \"Buy milk\",\n  completed: false,\n})\n```\n\nThese operations:\n\n- Write directly to the synced data store\n- Do NOT create optimistic mutations\n- Return a promise that resolves when the sync commit is accepted, including any configured persistence write. Await it in a mutation handler before returning so the server response is stored before the optimistic state drops.\n- While a mutation handler is running, wait behind it and become visible when its optimistic transaction settles, in the same update that drops its optimistic state\n- In eager mode, update the full-result TanStack Query cache in place without refetching\n- In on-demand mode, refetch active enabled queries and remove inactive or disabled cache entries\n\nThe promise does not wait for on-demand Query revalidation. If a handler returns before it writes the server response, its optimistic state drops and the row shows the previous server value until the response arrives.\nAfter Collection cleanup starts, direct writes fail with `SyncNotInitializedError` until a new sync run starts.\n\n### Batch Operations\n\nThe `writeBatch` method allows you to perform multiple operations atomically. Any write operations called within the callback will be collected and executed as a single transaction:\n\nThe batch includes writes to this Collection only. A synchronous callback may also write to another Collection, but that write has its own completion promise.\n\n```typescript\ntodosCollection.utils.writeBatch(() => {\n  todosCollection.utils.writeInsert({ id: \"1\", text: \"Buy milk\" })\n  todosCollection.utils.writeInsert({ id: \"2\", text: \"Walk dog\" })\n  todosCollection.utils.writeUpdate({ id: \"3\", completed: true })\n  todosCollection.utils.writeDelete(\"4\")\n})\n```\n\nAwait `writeBatch(...)` to wait for the whole sync commit. The callback must be synchronous; the promises returned by writes inside it settle with the batch.\n\n### Real-World Example: WebSocket Integration\n\nThis example assumes the collection stays loaded for the app's lifetime, so the\nlistener can live at module scope.\n\n```typescript\n// Apply real-time changes from a WebSocket as direct writes\nws.on(\"todos:update\", (changes) => {\n  todosCollection.utils.writeBatch(() => {\n    changes.forEach((change) => {\n      switch (change.type) {\n        case \"insert\":\n          todosCollection.utils.writeInsert(change.data)\n          break\n        case \"update\":\n          todosCollection.utils.writeUpdate(change.data)\n          break\n        case \"delete\":\n          todosCollection.utils.writeDelete(change.id)\n          break\n      }\n    })\n  })\n})\n```\n\n- In eager mode, these writes patch the cached query result without a\n  refetch. In on-demand mode, each direct write refetches the active queries.\n  See [Direct Writes and Query Sync](#direct-writes-and-query-sync).\n- A direct write is validated against the synced store after every earlier\n  sync commit is accepted. `writeUpdate` and `writeDelete` reject their\n  promise if the row is not in the synced store, and `writeInsert` rejects if\n  the row is already there. They do not throw. An event can arrive before the\n  first load finishes, or for a row the collection has not loaded. Handle the\n  rejection, or use `writeUpsert` when events carry the full row.\n- In a persisted collection, a direct write can wait for an earlier sync\n  commit's durable write, so its rows can appear after the call returns. Await\n  the returned promise before you read them.\n\nA direct write returns a promise. Await it to know that the write applied, and\ncatch its rejection to handle a validation error:\n\n```typescript\ntry {\n  await todosCollection.utils.writeUpdate({ id: change.id, done: true })\n} catch (error) {\n  if (error instanceof UpdateOperationItemNotFoundError) {\n    // The row is not in the synced store yet. Refetch, or skip this event.\n    await todosCollection.utils.refetch()\n  } else {\n    throw error\n  }\n}\n```\n\nImport `UpdateOperationItemNotFoundError`, `DeleteOperationItemNotFoundError`\nand `DuplicateKeyInBatchError` from `@tanstack/query-db-collection`, and\n`DuplicateKeySyncError` from `@tanstack/db`.\n\n### Example: Incremental Updates\n\nWhen the server returns computed fields (like server-generated IDs or timestamps), you can use direct writes to sync the server response without triggering a full refetch:\n\n```typescript\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: fetchTodos,\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n      const newItems = transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.modified)\n\n      // Send to server and get back items with server-computed fields\n      const serverItems = await api.createTodos(newItems)\n\n      // Sync server-computed fields (like server-generated IDs, timestamps, etc.)\n      // to the collection's synced data store using direct writes\n      await collection.utils.writeBatch(() => {\n        serverItems.forEach((serverItem) => {\n          collection.utils.writeInsert(serverItem)\n        })\n      })\n\n      // No need to refetch - we've already synced the server response via direct writes\n      // (optimistic state is automatically replaced when handler completes)\n      return { refetch: false } // Transitional pre-1.0 opt-out\n    },\n\n    onUpdate: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n      const updates = transaction.mutations.map((m) => ({\n        id: m.key,\n        changes: m.changes,\n      }))\n      const serverItems = await api.updateTodos(updates)\n\n      // Sync server-computed fields from the update response\n      await collection.utils.writeBatch(() => {\n        serverItems.forEach((serverItem) => {\n          collection.utils.writeUpdate(serverItem)\n        })\n      })\n\n      // No refetch needed since we used direct writes\n      return { refetch: false } // Transitional pre-1.0 opt-out\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n// Usage is just like a regular collection\ntodosCollection.insert({ text: \"Buy milk\", completed: false })\n```\n\n### Server pagination with live queries\n\n`useLiveInfiniteQuery` in React, Vue, and Svelte grows a local ordered query\nwindow. It does not run TanStack Query's `InfiniteQueryObserver`. Query\nCollections use `QueryObserver`, so `queryFn` receives\n`meta.loadSubsetOptions`, not `pageParam`.\n\nThe previously ignored `getNextPageParam` option has been removed. Delete it\nfrom your hook config; passing it at runtime now throws a clear error.\n`initialPageParam` labels result pages only. It does not set a remote offset\nor server cursor.\n\nFor server loading, use `syncMode: 'on-demand'` and make `queryFn` fulfill the\nrequested filter, order, offset, and limit. Use a deterministic total order\n(for example, a timestamp followed by a unique ID). The loader may request a\nprefix, a suffix, a tie group, or the full filtered source. A request is not\nnecessarily one UI page: the hook fetches an extra row to determine\n`hasNextPage`. Returning one capped endpoint page can incorrectly make the\nquery appear exhausted even when the server has more rows.\n\n#### Endpoints with fixed-size pages\n\nIf your endpoint uses page numbers, drain enough server pages to fulfill each\nrequest. This example assumes a zero-based page API with a fixed size of 50.\nThe endpoint must apply the supplied filters and sorts **before** pagination,\nkeep a consistent ordered result while its pages are read, and return\n`nextPage: null` only when it has authoritatively exhausted that result.\nThis example uses offset-based pagination. `api.listPosts` translates the full\n`where` expression and `orderBy` options into the endpoint's syntax, and rejects\nunsupported expressions. The separate `cursor` hints are deliberately unused;\ncursor-based adapters must handle those hints alongside `where`, not treat them\nas already included in it. See [QueryFn and Predicate Push-Down](#queryfn-and-predicate-push-down)\nfor translation helpers. Do not drop predicates or filter after paginating:\neither changes the requested window.\n\n```typescript\nimport { createCollection } from '@tanstack/db'\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from '@tanstack/query-db-collection'\n\ntype Post = { id: number; createdAt: number; title: string }\nconst serverPageSize = 50\n\nconst postsCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: ['posts'],\n    queryClient,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    getKey: (post: Post) => post.id,\n    queryFn: async (ctx): Promise<Array<Post>> => {\n      const { where, orderBy, offset = 0, limit } = ctx.meta?.loadSubsetOptions ?? {}\n      const skip = offset % serverPageSize\n      let page: number | null = Math.floor(offset / serverPageSize)\n      const gathered: Array<Post> = []\n\n      while (page !== null && (limit === undefined || gathered.length < skip + limit)) {\n        ctx.signal.throwIfAborted()\n        const response: { rows: Array<Post>; nextPage: number | null } =\n          await api.listPosts({\n            page,\n            pageSize: serverPageSize,\n            where,\n            orderBy,\n            signal: ctx.signal,\n          })\n        gathered.push(...response.rows)\n        page = response.nextPage\n      }\n\n      return gathered.slice(skip, limit === undefined ? undefined : skip + limit)\n    },\n  }),\n)\n\n// React example; the collection protocol is the same for Vue and Svelte.\nconst { data, fetchNextPage, hasNextPage } = useLiveInfiniteQuery(\n  (q) => q.from({ post: postsCollection })\n    .orderBy(({ post }) => post.createdAt)\n    .orderBy(({ post }) => post.id),\n  { pageSize: 20 },\n)\n```\n\nReject failed requests instead of returning partial rows as success. An\nunlimited request must drain until the endpoint reports exhaustion. For an\nendpoint with opaque cursors, use `createCursorPager` as shown below. The hook\ndoes not maintain remote cursor history on its own.\n\nManually appending rows with `writeUpsert` is a separate, lower-level loading\nstrategy. It does not make an eager `queryFn` incremental: a later successful\nrefetch still replaces its complete state and can remove appended rows.\n`staleTime: Infinity` does not prevent explicit refetch or invalidation.\n\n#### Endpoints with opaque cursors\n\nQuery Collections ask for arbitrary windows, such as rows 40–59. An\nopaque-cursor API cannot jump to row 40. It can only fetch the first page, then\nfollow the continuation token returned with each page.\n\n`createCursorPager` bridges those two protocols. It stores complete backend\npages in your existing QueryClient and follows their cursors until it has enough\nrows for the requested `offset` and `limit`. A later request can reuse that\nfresh run of pages and fetch only the missing suffix. The hook still requests\nan extra row to decide `hasNextPage`; the pager just fulfills the resulting\nwindow.\n\n```typescript\nimport { createCollection } from '@tanstack/db'\nimport { createCursorPager, queryCollectionOptions } from '@tanstack/query-db-collection'\n\ntype Post = { id: number; createdAt: number; title: string }\n\nconst postsCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryClient,\n    queryKey: ['posts', 'rows'],\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    getKey: (post: Post) => post.id,\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      const { where, orderBy, offset = 0, limit } = ctx.meta?.loadSubsetOptions ?? {}\n\n      // Translate every filter and sort, or reject unsupported expressions.\n      // This stable, serializable value identifies one ordered result sequence.\n      const request = api.translatePostQuery({ where, orderBy })\n\n      const pager = createCursorPager<Post>({\n        queryClient,\n        queryKey: ['posts', 'cursor-pages', request],\n        staleTime: 60_000,\n        gcTime: 5 * 60_000,\n        fetchPage: async (cursor, signal) => {\n          const response = await api.listPosts({ ...request, cursor, signal })\n\n          // Only null means that the source is exhausted.\n          return { rows: response.items, nextCursor: response.nextCursor ?? null }\n        },\n      })\n\n      return pager.read({ offset, limit }, ctx.signal)\n    },\n  }),\n)\n```\n\n##### Give each result sequence its own key\n\nThe pager's query key identifies one ordered backend result, not one requested\nwindow. Leave `offset` and `limit` out of the key so wider reads can extend the\nsame cached prefix. Include everything that changes the result—such as the\ntenant, source, filters, and order.\n\nKeep row data and cursor pages under sibling keys:\n\n```text\n['posts', 'rows']                         QueryCollection row array\n['posts', 'cursor-pages', request]        backend pages and cursors\n```\n\nThey cannot share a key because the two cache entries have different shapes.\nDo not put cursor pages beneath the row key either: manual collection writes\ntarget entries under the row prefix. A shared resource prefix such as\n`['posts']` still lets you cancel or invalidate both kinds of data together.\n\nThe example creates a small pager object for each `queryFn` call. Those objects\nstill share cached pages and in-flight fetches through QueryClient. Each pager\nserializes its own reads. Retain one pager per result sequence only if calls\nmust also share that queue or you need to call `pager.reset()` later.\n\n##### What happens when the collection asks for rows\n\nFor fresh data, the pager uses every cached page it can and fetches only the\nmissing suffix. `nextCursor: null` is the sole exhaustion signal: a short or\nempty page with another cursor does not end the sequence. An omitted `limit`\ndrains the source; a zero limit performs no fetch. Repeated continuation tokens\nthrow instead of looping forever.\n\nQuery owns freshness, garbage collection, retries, and invalidation. Pass\n`staleTime` and `gcTime` to the pager or let it inherit QueryClient defaults.\nConfigure retries through QueryClient defaults for the page-key prefix;\nimperative Query fetches do not retry by default. The pager keeps every page in\nits original form, so Query defaults for `maxPages` and `select` do not apply.\n\nWhen cached pages are stale, the next read refetches every page loaded so far,\nstarting with the first. It does this even for a shallow window because old\ncontinuation tokens belong to the old sequence. Staleness does not start a\nbackground timer. The page query has no lasting observer, so its `gcTime` may\nexpire while the collection's published rows remain visible.\n\nA failed read rejects rather than returning a partial window. Query keeps the\nlast successful pages, so a later read can retry. Treat their rows as immutable,\njust like other Query data. `read()` returns references from the cached pages,\nnot detached copies, and does not promise the same array or object identity\nacross calls.\n\n##### Refresh or cancel the sequence\n\nAfter a mutation—or whenever you need a forced refresh—cancel the shared\nresource prefix before invalidating it:\n\n```typescript\nawait queryClient.cancelQueries({ queryKey: ['posts'] })\nawait queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['posts'] })\n```\n\nCancelling first stops an old page append from completing after invalidation and\nmarking the old sequence fresh again. It rejects readers waiting on that fetch\nwith an `AbortError` and prevents a late result from entering the cache. The\nbackend must honor the signal if you also want to stop its network work.\n\nAborting the signal passed to `read()` rejects only that reader; it does not\ncancel a page fetch shared with another reader. A cancelling Query refetch moves\nwaiting readers to the replacement fetch. `collection.utils.refetch()` refreshes\nthe row queries but may reuse fresh cursor pages. `pager.reset()` removes one\npager's exact page key and cancels its queued reads, but does not refresh the\ncollection's row query.\n\n##### Backend contract\n\nThe helper handles cursor traversal only. Your adapter must:\n\n- translate every requested `where` and `orderBy`, or reject unsupported\n  expressions;\n- apply filters and ordering before pagination;\n- use a deterministic total order, including a unique tie-breaker;\n- keep that ordered result consistent while its cursor sequence is read; and\n- return `nextCursor: null` only when the result is authoritatively exhausted.\n\n`LoadSubsetOptions.cursor` contains query-expression hints, not the backend's\nopaque token. `createCursorPager` therefore addresses the cursor sequence by\noffset and deliberately does not consume those hints. No client-side pager can\nmake a stable snapshot from an endpoint whose ordering changes between pages;\nthe endpoint must define its own consistency and cursor-expiry rules.\n\n## Important Behaviors\n\n### Full State Sync\n\nThe query collection treats the `queryFn` result as the **complete state** of the collection. This means:\n\n- Items present in the collection but not in the query result will be deleted\n- Items in the query result but not in the collection will be inserted\n- Items present in both will be updated if they differ\n\nThis is important when the same entity type can be loaded from multiple REST\nendpoints. For example, do not point the same Query Collection at\n`/api/documents/preview` for one load and `/api/documents/deleted` for another\nunless each result represents the complete state for that collection\nscope. A narrower endpoint can otherwise remove rows that were loaded from a\ndifferent endpoint.\n\nFor multiple endpoint or subset-loading use cases, choose the pattern that\nmatches your API semantics:\n\n- Use `syncMode: 'on-demand'` when one logical collection can serve different\n  subsets of data. In this mode, query predicates (`where`, `orderBy`, `limit`,\n  and `offset`) are passed to your `queryFn` via `ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions`,\n  letting you translate them into API parameters.\n- Use separate Query Collections when endpoints represent distinct server scopes\n  whose results should not replace each other. Use `unionAll` to combine them\n  into a single query when you need a unified view across endpoints.\n- Use direct writes such as `writeUpsert`/`writeBatch` for lower-level\n  incremental loading when you intentionally want to merge server responses into\n  the synced store yourself.\n\n### Empty Array Behavior\n\nWhen `queryFn` returns an empty array, **all items in the collection will be deleted**. This is because the collection interprets an empty array as \"the server has no items\".\n\n```typescript\n// This will delete all items in the collection\nqueryFn: async () => []\n```\n\n### Handling Partial/Incremental Fetches\n\nSince the query collection expects `queryFn` to return the complete state, you can handle partial fetches by merging new data with existing data:\n\n```typescript\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async ({ queryKey }) => {\n      // Get existing data from cache\n      const existingData = queryClient.getQueryData(queryKey) || []\n\n      // Fetch only new/updated items (e.g., changes since last sync)\n      const lastSyncTime = localStorage.getItem(\"todos-last-sync\")\n      const newData = await fetch(`/api/todos?since=${lastSyncTime}`).then(\n        (r) => r.json()\n      )\n\n      // Merge new data with existing data\n      const existingMap = new Map(existingData.map((item) => [item.id, item]))\n\n      // Apply updates and additions\n      newData.forEach((item) => {\n        existingMap.set(item.id, item)\n      })\n\n      // Handle deletions if your API provides them\n      if (newData.deletions) {\n        newData.deletions.forEach((id) => existingMap.delete(id))\n      }\n\n      // Update sync time\n      localStorage.setItem(\"todos-last-sync\", new Date().toISOString())\n\n      // Return the complete merged state\n      return Array.from(existingMap.values())\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  })\n)\n```\n\nThis pattern allows you to:\n\n- Fetch only incremental changes from your API\n- Merge those changes with existing data\n- Return the complete state that the collection expects\n- Avoid the performance overhead of fetching all data every time\n\n### Direct Writes and Query Sync\n\nDirect writes update the collection immediately, or when a running mutation handler settles. In eager mode, they also patch the full-result TanStack Query cache in place.\n\nIn on-demand mode, each Query cache entry may represent a different predicate, order, limit, or offset. A full collection snapshot cannot safely replace those scoped results. Direct writes therefore refetch active enabled queries and remove inactive or disabled entries. A successful `queryFn` result remains authoritative and may reconcile or replace a direct write.\n\nTo handle this properly:\n\n1. Use `{ refetch: false }` in persistence handlers to avoid the handler's additional refetch after a direct write. On-demand cache revalidation still runs.\n2. Make sure an on-demand `queryFn` returns the current server result for its pushed-down predicate, order, limit, and offset.\n3. Use eager mode when direct writes must update one complete cached result without a network request.\n\n## Complete Direct Write API Reference\n\nAll direct write methods are available on `collection.utils`:\n\n- `writeInsert(data)`: Insert one or more items directly\n- `writeUpdate(data)`: Update one or more items directly\n- `writeDelete(keys)`: Delete one or more items directly\n- `writeUpsert(data)`: Insert or update one or more items directly\n- `writeBatch(callback)`: Perform multiple operations atomically\n- `refetch(opts?)`: Trigger a refetch of the query\n\n## QueryFn and Predicate Push-Down\n\nWhen using `syncMode: 'on-demand'`, the collection automatically pushes down query predicates (where clauses, orderBy, limit, and offset) to your `queryFn`. This allows you to fetch only the data needed for each specific query, rather than fetching the entire dataset.\n\n### How LoadSubsetOptions Are Passed\n\nLoadSubsetOptions are passed to your `queryFn` via the query context's `meta` property:\n\n```typescript\nqueryFn: async (ctx) => {\n  // Extract LoadSubsetOptions from the context\n  const { limit, offset, where, orderBy } = ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions\n\n  // Use these to fetch only the data you need\n  // - where: filter expression (AST)\n  // - orderBy: sort expression (AST)\n  // - limit: maximum number of rows\n  // - offset: number of rows to skip (for pagination)\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nThe `where` and `orderBy` fields are expression trees (AST - Abstract Syntax Tree) that need to be parsed. TanStack DB provides helper functions to make this easy.\n\n### Expression Helpers\n\n```typescript\nimport {\n  parseWhereExpression,\n  parseOrderByExpression,\n  extractSimpleComparisons,\n  parseLoadSubsetOptions,\n} from '@tanstack/db'\n// Or from '@tanstack/query-db-collection' (re-exported for convenience)\n```\n\nThese helpers allow you to parse expression trees without manually traversing complex AST structures.\n\n### Quick Start: Simple REST API\n\n```typescript\nimport { createCollection } from '@tanstack/react-db'\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from '@tanstack/query-db-collection'\nimport { parseLoadSubsetOptions } from '@tanstack/db'\nimport { QueryClient } from '@tanstack/query-core'\n\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient()\n\nconst productsCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    id: 'products',\n    queryKey: ['products'],\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand', // Enable predicate push-down\n\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      const { limit, offset, where, orderBy } = ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions\n\n      // Parse the expressions into simple format\n      const parsed = parseLoadSubsetOptions({ where, orderBy, limit })\n\n      // Build query parameters from parsed filters\n      const params = new URLSearchParams()\n\n      // Add filters\n      parsed.filters.forEach(({ field, operator, value }) => {\n        const fieldName = field.join('.')\n        if (operator === 'eq') {\n          params.set(fieldName, String(value))\n        } else if (operator === 'lt') {\n          params.set(`${fieldName}_lt`, String(value))\n        } else if (operator === 'gt') {\n          params.set(`${fieldName}_gt`, String(value))\n        }\n      })\n\n      // Add sorting\n      if (parsed.sorts.length > 0) {\n        const sortParam = parsed.sorts\n          .map(s => `${s.field.join('.')}:${s.direction}`)\n          .join(',')\n        params.set('sort', sortParam)\n      }\n\n      // Add limit\n      if (parsed.limit) {\n        params.set('limit', String(parsed.limit))\n      }\n\n      // Add offset for pagination\n      if (offset) {\n        params.set('offset', String(offset))\n      }\n\n      const response = await fetch(`/api/products?${params}`)\n      return response.json()\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n// Usage with live queries\nimport { createLiveQueryCollection } from '@tanstack/react-db'\nimport { eq, lt, and } from '@tanstack/db'\n\nconst affordableElectronics = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q.from({ product: productsCollection })\n     .where(({ product }) => and(\n       eq(product.category, 'electronics'),\n       lt(product.price, 100)\n     ))\n     .orderBy(({ product }) => product.price, 'asc')\n     .limit(10)\n     .select(({ product }) => product)\n})\n\n// This triggers a queryFn call with:\n// GET /api/products?category=electronics&price_lt=100&sort=price:asc&limit=10\n// When paginating, offset is included: &offset=20\n```\n\n### Custom Handlers for Complex APIs\n\nFor APIs with specific formats, use custom handlers:\n\n```typescript\nqueryFn: async (ctx) => {\n  const { where, orderBy, limit } = ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions\n\n  // Use custom handlers to match your API's format\n  const filters = parseWhereExpression(where, {\n    handlers: {\n      eq: (field, value) => ({\n        field: field.join('.'),\n        op: 'equals',\n        value\n      }),\n      lt: (field, value) => ({\n        field: field.join('.'),\n        op: 'lessThan',\n        value\n      }),\n      and: (...conditions) => ({\n        operator: 'AND',\n        conditions\n      }),\n      or: (...conditions) => ({\n        operator: 'OR',\n        conditions\n      }),\n    }\n  })\n\n  const sorts = parseOrderByExpression(orderBy)\n\n  return api.query({\n    filters,\n    sort: sorts.map(s => ({\n      field: s.field.join('.'),\n      order: s.direction.toUpperCase()\n    })),\n    limit\n  })\n}\n```\n\n### GraphQL Example\n\n```typescript\nqueryFn: async (ctx) => {\n  const { where, orderBy, limit } = ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions\n\n  // Convert to a GraphQL where clause format\n  const whereClause = parseWhereExpression(where, {\n    handlers: {\n      eq: (field, value) => ({\n        [field.join('_')]: { _eq: value }\n      }),\n      lt: (field, value) => ({\n        [field.join('_')]: { _lt: value }\n      }),\n      and: (...conditions) => ({ _and: conditions }),\n      or: (...conditions) => ({ _or: conditions }),\n    }\n  })\n\n  // Convert to a GraphQL order_by format\n  const sorts = parseOrderByExpression(orderBy)\n  const orderByClause = sorts.map(s => ({\n    [s.field.join('_')]: s.direction\n  }))\n\n  const { data } = await graphqlClient.query({\n    query: gql`\n      query GetProducts($where: product_bool_exp, $orderBy: [product_order_by!], $limit: Int) {\n        product(where: $where, order_by: $orderBy, limit: $limit) {\n          id\n          name\n          category\n          price\n        }\n      }\n    `,\n    variables: {\n      where: whereClause,\n      orderBy: orderByClause,\n      limit\n    }\n  })\n\n  return data.product\n}\n```\n\n### Expression Helper API Reference\n\n#### `parseLoadSubsetOptions(options)`\n\nConvenience function that parses all LoadSubsetOptions at once. Good for simple use cases.\n\n```typescript\nconst { filters, sorts, limit, offset } = parseLoadSubsetOptions(ctx.meta?.loadSubsetOptions)\n// filters: [{ field: ['category'], operator: 'eq', value: 'electronics' }]\n// sorts: [{ field: ['price'], direction: 'asc', nulls: 'last' }]\n// limit: 10\n// offset: 20 (for pagination)\n```\n\n#### `parseWhereExpression(expr, options)`\n\nParses a WHERE expression using custom handlers for each operator. Use this for complete control over the output format.\n\n```typescript\nconst filters = parseWhereExpression(where, {\n  handlers: {\n    eq: (field, value) => ({ [field.join('.')]: value }),\n    lt: (field, value) => ({ [`${field.join('.')}_lt`]: value }),\n    and: (...filters) => Object.assign({}, ...filters)\n  },\n  onUnknownOperator: (operator, args) => {\n    console.warn(`Unsupported operator: ${operator}`)\n    return null\n  }\n})\n```\n\n#### `parseOrderByExpression(orderBy)`\n\nParses an ORDER BY expression into a simple array.\n\n```typescript\nconst sorts = parseOrderByExpression(orderBy)\n// Returns: [{ field: ['price'], direction: 'asc', nulls: 'last' }]\n```\n\n#### `extractSimpleComparisons(expr)`\n\nExtracts simple AND-ed comparisons from a WHERE expression. Note: Only works for simple AND conditions.\n\n```typescript\nconst comparisons = extractSimpleComparisons(where)\n// Returns: [\n//   { field: ['category'], operator: 'eq', value: 'electronics' },\n//   { field: ['price'], operator: 'lt', value: 100 }\n// ]\n```\n\n### Supported Operators\n\n- `eq` - Equality (=)\n- `gt` - Greater than (>)\n- `gte` - Greater than or equal (>=)\n- `lt` - Less than (<)\n- `lte` - Less than or equal (<=)\n- `and` - Logical AND\n- `or` - Logical OR\n- `in` - IN clause\n\n### Using Query Key Builders\n\nWith `syncMode: 'on-demand'`, a static key such as `queryKey: ['products']`\nautomatically includes the subset demand identity. Filters, ordering, limits,\noffsets, and cursor hints distinguish cache entries when they change the\nrequested data. Prefer a static key when you do not need a custom key structure.\n\nA function-based `queryKey` replaces that automatic key construction. Include\nall options that change the requested data; omitting `offset`, for example,\ncan make different windows share a cache entry. Use `getLoadSubsetDemandKey`\nfrom `@tanstack/db` to retain the same subset identity as a static key, including\n`limit: 0`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { getLoadSubsetDemandKey } from '@tanstack/db'\n\nconst productsCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    id: 'products',\n    queryKey: (opts) => {\n      const demandKey = getLoadSubsetDemandKey(opts)\n      return demandKey === undefined ? ['products'] : ['products', demandKey]\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => { /* ... */ },\n  })\n)\n```\n\n#### Query Key Prefix Convention\n\nWhen using a function-based `queryKey`, all derived keys **must extend the base key as a prefix**. The base key is what your function returns when called with no options (`queryKey({})`).\n\nTanStack Query uses prefix matching for cache operations internally. The query collection relies on this to find all cache entries belonging to a collection — including stale entries from destroyed query observers that are still held in cache due to `gcTime`. If derived keys don't share the base prefix, cache updates may silently miss entries, leading to stale data.\n\nThe example above returns `['products']` for `queryKey({})` and keeps it as\nthe prefix of every derived key. Avoid changing the prefix for subset demands:\n\n```typescript\n// ❌ Wrong: base key ['products-all'] is NOT a prefix of ['products-filtered', ...]\nqueryKey: (opts) => {\n  const demandKey = getLoadSubsetDemandKey(opts)\n  return demandKey === undefined\n    ? ['products-all']\n    : ['products-filtered', demandKey]\n}\n```\n\n### Tips\n\n1. **Start with `parseLoadSubsetOptions`** for simple use cases\n2. **Use custom handlers** via `parseWhereExpression` for APIs with specific formats\n3. **Handle unsupported operators** with the `onUnknownOperator` callback\n4. **Log parsed results** during development to verify correctness\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Query Collection\n---\n\nQuery collections provide seamless integration between TanStack DB and TanStack Query, enabling automatic synchronization between your local database and remote data sources.\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `@tanstack/query-db-collection` package allows you to create collections that:\n\n- Automatically fetch remote data via TanStack Query\n- Support optimistic updates with automatic rollback on errors\n- Handle persistence through customizable mutation handlers\n- Provide direct write capabilities for directly writing to the sync store\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\nnpm install @tanstack/query-db-collection @tanstack/query-core @tanstack/db\n```\n\n## Basic Usage\n\n```typescript\nimport { QueryClient } from \"@tanstack/query-core\"\nimport { DbClient, collectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/db\"\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\"\n\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient()\nconst db = new DbClient({ queryClient })\n\nconst todosCollection = collectionOptions(\"todos\", (client) =>\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    id: \"todos\",\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async () => {\n      const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\")\n      return response.json()\n    },\n    queryClient: client.requireDependency<QueryClient>(\"queryClient\"),\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  })\n)\n\nconst todos = db.collection(todosCollection)\n```\n\n## Configuration Options\n\nThe `queryCollectionOptions` function accepts the following options:\n\n### Required Options\n\n- `queryKey`: The query key for TanStack Query. Can be a static array or a function that receives `LoadSubsetOptions` and returns a key. When using a function, all returned keys must share the base key (`queryKey({})`) as a prefix — see [Query Key Prefix Convention](#query-key-prefix-convention).\n- `queryFn`: Function that fetches data from the server\n- `queryClient`: TanStack Query client instance\n- `getKey`: Function to extract the unique key from an item\n\n### Request-scoped QueryClient\n\n`queryCollectionOptions` needs a `queryClient`. In SSR, TanStack Start, tests,\nor multi-tenant apps, that client is request-local rather than module-global.\nPut it on `DbClient`, then resolve it inside the collection descriptor factory:\n\n```typescript\nimport { QueryClient } from \"@tanstack/query-core\"\nimport { DbClient, collectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/db\"\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\"\n\ninterface Todo {\n  id: string\n  title: string\n}\n\nexport const todoCollection = collectionOptions(\"todos\", (client) =>\n  queryCollectionOptions<Todo>({\n    id: \"todos\",\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async () => {\n      const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\")\n      return response.json() as Promise<Array<Todo>>\n    },\n    queryClient: client.requireDependency<QueryClient>(\"queryClient\"),\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n  })\n)\n\nexport function createRequestClients() {\n  const queryClient = new QueryClient()\n  const dbClient = new DbClient({ queryClient })\n  return { queryClient, dbClient }\n}\n```\n\n`dbClient.collection(todoCollection)` memoizes one collection instance for that\ndescriptor and client. A second `DbClient` materializes fresh adapter state and\nuses its own `QueryClient`.\n\nPassing `queryClient` directly to `queryCollectionOptions` remains supported for\n`createCollection(...)` and existing apps. When a descriptor is materialized,\nan explicit `DbClient` dependency takes precedence; the configured\n`queryClient` is the backwards-compatible fallback.\n\n### Business-Scoped Collection Factories\n\nA tenant, project, account, or route parameter can define a **business scope**:\nthe server resource that a collection represents. Include the scope in the\ndescriptor id, Query key, and `queryFn`. This extends the\n[request-scoped QueryClient pattern](#request-scoped-queryclient) with an\nexplicit scope parameter:\n\n```typescript\ninterface Todo {\n  id: string\n  title: string\n  projectId: string\n}\n\nasync function fetchProjectTodos(projectId: string): Promise<Array<Todo>> {\n  const response = await fetch(`/api/projects/${projectId}/todos`)\n  return response.json()\n}\n\nfunction createProjectTodosDescriptor(\n  projectId: string,\n) {\n  return collectionOptions(`project:${projectId}:todos`, (client) =>\n    queryCollectionOptions<Todo>({\n      id: `project:${projectId}:todos`,\n      queryKey: [\"projects\", projectId, \"todos\"],\n      queryFn: () => fetchProjectTodos(projectId),\n      queryClient: client.requireDependency<QueryClient>(\"queryClient\"),\n      getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    })\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe scope is part of the descriptor identity. `DbClient` resolves separately\ncreated descriptors with the same id to the same collection, so a React hook\ncan create the descriptor from its current parameters:\n\n```typescript\nexport function useProjectTodos(projectId: string) {\n  return useDbClient().collection(createProjectTodosDescriptor(projectId))\n}\n```\n\nOnly the first descriptor for an id is materialized. Include every scope value\nthat changes the collection in both its descriptor id and Query key. Call\n`await dbClient.cleanup()` when the client scope ends.\n\nA business scope is separate from a **relational subset** requested by a live query. With `syncMode: \"on-demand\"`, `LoadSubsetOptions` describes predicates, ordering, limits, and offsets within one business-scoped collection. These options reach `queryFn` through `ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions` and determine the subset Query keys. See [QueryFn and Predicate Push-Down](#queryfn-and-predicate-push-down).\n\nDo not create a collection for each `where`, `orderBy`, or `limit`. Reuse the business-scoped collection and let on-demand loading represent those subsets. Create separate collections only for distinct server resources.\n\n### Query Options\n\nQuery Collections use TanStack Query internally and expose supported Query observer options as top-level `queryCollectionOptions` fields.\n\nThe following top-level Query Collection options are forwarded to the underlying Query observer:\n\n- `select`: Function that extracts the row array TanStack DB materializes from a wrapped Query response\n- `enabled`: Whether the query should automatically run (default: `true`)\n- `refetchInterval`: Refetch interval in milliseconds\n- `retry`: Retry configuration for failed queries\n- `retryDelay`: Delay between retries\n- `staleTime`: How long data is considered fresh\n- `gcTime`: How long unused query data stays in the Query cache\n- `refetchOnWindowFocus`: Whether to refetch when the window regains focus\n- `refetchOnReconnect`: Whether to refetch when the network reconnects\n- `refetchOnMount`: Whether to refetch when the observer mounts\n- `networkMode`: Query network mode\n- `initialData`: Initial Query response for eager collections\n- `initialDataUpdatedAt`: Timestamp used by TanStack Query to determine initial data freshness\n- `meta`: Metadata passed to the query function context. Query Collections may add `loadSubsetOptions` for on-demand queries.\n\n```ts\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: fetchTodos,\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    refetchOnWindowFocus: true,\n    refetchOnReconnect: true,\n    refetchOnMount: \"always\",\n    networkMode: \"online\",\n  })\n)\n```\n\nTop-level `meta` is always merged by Query Collection so it can add on-demand `loadSubsetOptions`. Other supported top-level Query options are only passed to TanStack Query when you define them. If you omit them, `QueryClient.defaultOptions` can still apply.\n\nSome fields are owned or reinterpreted by the collection adapter rather than treated as ordinary Query option pass-through:\n\n- `queryKey`: Identifies the Query cache entry and, in on-demand mode, may be built from load-subset options.\n- `queryFn`: Fetches the complete collection state or the requested on-demand subset.\n- `select`: Extracts array rows from wrapped responses before they are stored in the collection. This is not the same contract as TanStack Query's `select` option.\n- `queryClient`: Supplies the Query client instance used by the collection.\n- `syncMode`: Controls whether the collection syncs eagerly or on demand.\n- `getKey`: Extracts each row's stable TanStack DB key.\n- Mutation handlers such as `onInsert`, `onUpdate`, and `onDelete`.\n\nSome TanStack Query fields are owned or reinterpreted by Query Collection and are intentionally not exposed as ordinary Query observer options:\n\n- `queryKey`, `queryFn`, and `queryClient`\n- `select` (Query Collection uses this for row extraction, not TanStack Query's observer-level `select` contract)\n- `meta` (merged by Query Collection so on-demand `loadSubsetOptions` can be included)\n- `subscribed` (Query Collection owns the observer subscription lifecycle)\n- `structuralSharing` and `notifyOnChangeProps` (managed by Query Collection synchronization)\n\n`placeholderData` is intentionally unsupported. TanStack Query treats placeholder data as observer-local presentation state rather than cached Query data. Materializing it would expose temporary UI data as collection-wide normalized rows. Render placeholders in the consuming UI instead.\n\n### Request Cancellation with `QueryFunctionContext.signal`\n\nTanStack Query passes an `AbortSignal` to `queryFn` through the query function\ncontext. Forward `ctx.signal` to `fetch` or another abortable client to make the\nrequest cancellable:\n\n```typescript\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\", {\n        signal: ctx.signal,\n      })\n\n      if (!response.ok) {\n        throw new Error(\"Failed to fetch todos\")\n      }\n\n      return response.json() as Promise<Array<Todo>>\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n  }),\n)\n```\n\nExplicit collection cleanup cancels each exact Query key the collection is\ncurrently tracking before removing it from the Query cache:\n\n```typescript\nawait todosCollection.cleanup()\n```\n\nThe underlying request is aborted only when its client consumes `ctx.signal`.\nA client that ignores the signal may continue its request even though the\ncollection has been cleaned up.\n\nAn unloaded on-demand subset is no longer tracked. A later explicit collection\ncleanup does not revisit its Query key.\n\nQuery cache entries are shared within a `QueryClient`. Explicit cleanup can\naffect other consumers using the same exact Query keys.\n\nOn-demand subset unloading does not explicitly call\n`queryClient.cancelQueries()`. It removes the subset's Query observer. If this\nwas the final observer and the query function consumed `ctx.signal`, TanStack\nQuery aborts the request. If the signal was ignored, or another observer still\nuses the same exact Query key, the request may finish and remain cached until\n`gcTime`.\n\n### Using with `queryOptions(...)`\n\nIf your app already uses TanStack Query's `queryOptions` helper (e.g. from `@tanstack/react-query`), you can spread compatible top-level options into `queryCollectionOptions`. Note that `queryFn` must be explicitly provided since query collections require it both in types and at runtime, and Query Collection's `select` option is for row extraction rather than TanStack Query observer-level selection:\n\n```typescript\nimport { QueryClient } from \"@tanstack/query-core\"\nimport { DbClient, collectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/db\"\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\"\nimport { queryOptions } from \"@tanstack/react-query\"\n\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient()\nconst db = new DbClient({ queryClient })\n\nconst listOptions = queryOptions({\n  queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n  queryFn: async () => {\n    const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\")\n    return response.json() as Promise<Array<{ id: string; title: string }>>\n  },\n})\n\nconst todosCollection = collectionOptions(\"todos\", (client) =>\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    id: \"todos\",\n    ...listOptions,\n    queryFn: (context) => listOptions.queryFn!(context),\n    queryClient: client.requireDependency<QueryClient>(\"queryClient\"),\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  }),\n)\n\nconst todos = db.collection(todosCollection)\n```\n\nIf `queryFn` is missing at runtime, `queryCollectionOptions` throws `QueryFnRequiredError`.\n\n### Initial Data\n\nEager Query Collections support TanStack Query's `initialData` and\n`initialDataUpdatedAt` options. Initial data has the original Query response\nshape, is stored in the Query cache, and is immediately materialized as\nnormalized collection rows. TanStack Query uses `initialDataUpdatedAt` together\nwith `staleTime` to decide whether to fetch.\n\n```typescript\nconst serverRenderedAt = Date.now()\nconst initialTodos = [\n  { id: \"1\", title: \"Write documentation\" },\n  { id: \"2\", title: \"Ship initial data support\" },\n]\n\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: fetchTodos,\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    initialData: initialTodos,\n    initialDataUpdatedAt: serverRenderedAt,\n    staleTime: 60_000,\n  }),\n)\n```\n\nAn existing cached or hydrated Query response takes precedence over\n`initialData`. Query keys remain the cache identity: two collections using the\nsame QueryClient and exact Query key observe one shared Query document, and a\nlater collection's initializer does not replace it. Use distinct Query keys for\nindependent documents.\n\nInitial data is supported only for eager collections. A collection-wide value\ncannot establish row membership for arbitrary on-demand predicates, ordering,\nlimits, and offsets. For `syncMode: \"on-demand\"`, seed or hydrate the exact\nderived Query cache entries instead.\n\nIf a stale initial response triggers a fetch, the initial rows remain available\nwhile it is in flight. A successful response reconciles them through the normal\nrow ownership pipeline; an error retains the initial rows. Direct writes patch\nthe eager Query cache in place. On-demand direct writes revalidate scoped\nentries as described below.\n\n### Selecting Rows from Wrapped Responses\n\nMany APIs return rows inside a response envelope that also contains metadata such as pagination cursors, totals, or request information. Use `select` to extract the row array that TanStack DB should materialize:\n\n```typescript\ninterface TodosResponse {\n  items: Array<{ id: string; title: string }>\n  nextCursor?: string\n  total: number\n}\n\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async (): Promise<TodosResponse> => {\n      const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\")\n      return response.json()\n    },\n    initialData: {\n      items: [{ id: \"1\", title: \"Initial todo\" }],\n      nextCursor: undefined,\n      total: 1,\n    },\n    select: (response) => response.items,\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  }),\n)\n```\n\n`select` is a query-db-collection row extraction hook. It tells TanStack DB which rows to materialize while the TanStack Query cache keeps the original query response shape. In the example above, `queryClient.getQueryData([\"todos\"])` still returns the full `TodosResponse`, including `nextCursor` and `total`.\n\nThe same projection applies to `initialData`: provide the complete response\nenvelope, and Query Collection materializes the rows returned by `select` while\npreserving the envelope in the Query cache.\n\nThis differs from TanStack Query's observer-level `select`: query-db-collection uses this option to bridge Query's response object into DB's normalized row store.\n\nIn eager mode, direct write utilities such as `writeInsert`, `writeUpdate`, and `writeDelete` make a best-effort attempt to update the matching row array inside wrapped Query cache entries while preserving wrapper metadata. In on-demand mode, they revalidate active scoped queries and remove inactive or disabled cache entries instead of patching them with the full collection snapshot.\n\nThis works automatically for simple wrappers such as:\n\n- `{ data: [...] }`\n- `{ items: [...] }`\n- `{ results: [...] }`\n\nDerived projections, such as `select: (response) => response.edges.map((edge) => edge.node)`, are read-side row extraction only. query-db-collection cannot generally reconstruct the original response envelope from updated rows. Refetch or invalidate the query if the wrapped cache must exactly reflect direct writes for a derived projection.\n\n### Collection Options\n\n- `id`: Unique identifier for the collection\n- `schema`: Schema for validating items\n- `sync`: Custom sync configuration\n- `startSync`: Whether to start syncing immediately (default: `true`)\n\n### Persistence Handlers\n\n- `onInsert`: Handler called before insert operations\n- `onUpdate`: Handler called before update operations\n- `onDelete`: Handler called before delete operations\n\n## Extending Meta with Custom Properties\n\nThe `meta` option allows you to pass additional metadata to your query function. By default, Query Collections automatically include `loadSubsetOptions` in the meta object, which contains filtering, sorting, and pagination options for on-demand queries.\n\n`loadSubsetOptions` is request-scoped. Query functions can read it through\n`ctx.meta`, but it is excluded when Query metadata is serialized for SSR.\nHydrated Query metadata therefore does not contain the server request options;\nnew on-demand requests supply their own options. Other enumerable `meta`\nproperties retain TanStack Query's normal dehydration behavior.\n\nTreat `ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions` and its nested request data as read-only.\nDo not edit expression nodes, ordering options, Dates, byte arrays, or membership\narrays. Build separate API parameters instead. Core retains request data without\ncloning it; changing submitted data can make the request disagree with its cache\nkey. To change a query constant, supply a new value rather than mutating the old\none. Cancellation through the request's `AbortSignal` remains supported.\n\n### Type-Safe Meta Access\n\nThe `ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions` property is automatically typed as `LoadSubsetOptions` without requiring any additional imports or type assertions:\n\n```typescript\nimport { parseLoadSubsetOptions } from \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\"\n\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"products\"],\n    syncMode: \"on-demand\",\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      // ✅ Type-safe access - no @ts-ignore needed!\n      const options = parseLoadSubsetOptions(ctx.meta?.loadSubsetOptions)\n\n      // Use the parsed options to fetch only what you need\n      return api.getProducts(options)\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  })\n)\n```\n\n### Adding Custom Meta Properties\n\nYou can extend the meta type to include your own custom properties using TypeScript's module augmentation:\n\n```typescript\n// In a global type definition file (e.g., types.d.ts or global.d.ts)\ndeclare module \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\" {\n  interface QueryCollectionMeta {\n    // Add your custom properties here\n    userId?: string\n    includeDeleted?: boolean\n    cacheTTL?: number\n  }\n}\n```\n\nOnce you've extended the interface, your custom properties are fully typed throughout your application:\n\n```typescript\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      // ✅ Both loadSubsetOptions and custom properties are typed\n      const { loadSubsetOptions, userId, includeDeleted } = ctx.meta\n\n      return api.getTodos({\n        ...parseLoadSubsetOptions(loadSubsetOptions),\n        userId,\n        includeDeleted,\n      })\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    // Pass custom meta alongside Query Collection defaults\n    meta: {\n      userId: \"user-123\",\n      includeDeleted: false,\n    },\n  })\n)\n```\n\n### Important Notes\n\n- The module augmentation pattern follows TanStack Query's official approach for typing meta\n- `QueryCollectionMeta` is an interface (not a type alias), enabling proper TypeScript declaration merging\n- Your custom properties are merged with the base `loadSubsetOptions` property\n- All meta properties must be compatible with `Record<string, unknown>`\n- The augmentation should be done in a file that's included in your TypeScript compilation\n\n### Example: API Request Context\n\nA common use case is passing request context to your query function:\n\n```typescript\n// types.d.ts\ndeclare module \"@tanstack/query-db-collection\" {\n  interface QueryCollectionMeta {\n    authToken?: string\n    locale?: string\n    version?: string\n  }\n}\n\n// collections.ts\nconst productsCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"products\"],\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      const { loadSubsetOptions, authToken, locale, version } = ctx.meta\n\n      return api.getProducts({\n        ...parseLoadSubsetOptions(loadSubsetOptions),\n        headers: {\n          Authorization: `Bearer ${authToken}`,\n          \"Accept-Language\": locale,\n          \"API-Version\": version,\n        },\n      })\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    meta: {\n      authToken: session.token,\n      locale: \"en-US\",\n      version: \"v1\",\n    },\n  })\n)\n```\n\n## Persistence Handlers\n\nYou can define handlers that are called when mutations occur. These handlers persist changes to your backend:\n\n```typescript\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: fetchTodos,\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      const newItems = transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.modified)\n      await api.createTodos(newItems)\n      // Auto-refetch happens after handler completes (pre-1.0 behavior)\n    },\n\n    onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      const updates = transaction.mutations.map((m) => ({\n        id: m.key,\n        changes: m.changes,\n      }))\n      await api.updateTodos(updates)\n    },\n\n    onDelete: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      const ids = transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.key)\n      await api.deleteTodos(ids)\n    },\n  })\n)\n```\n\n> **Note**: QueryCollection currently auto-refetches after handlers complete. See [Controlling Refetch Behavior](#controlling-refetch-behavior) for details on this transitional behavior.\n\n### Controlling Refetch Behavior\n\n> **⚠️ Transitional API**: QueryCollection currently auto-refetches after handlers complete. This behavior is deprecated and will be removed in v1.0. See the migration notes below.\n\n#### Current Behavior (Pre-1.0)\n\nBy default, QueryCollection automatically refetches after each handler completes. To **skip** auto-refetch, return `{ refetch: false }`:\n\n```typescript\nonInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n  await api.createTodos(transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.modified))\n\n  // Skip auto-refetch - use this when server doesn't modify the data\n  return { refetch: false }\n}\n```\n\nIf you don't return `{ refetch: false }`, auto-refetch happens automatically.\n\n#### v1.0 Behavior (Future)\n\nIn v1.0, auto-refetch will be **removed**. Handlers will need to explicitly call `collection.utils.refetch()` when refetching is needed:\n\n```typescript\nonInsert: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n  await api.createTodos(transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.modified))\n\n  // Explicitly trigger refetch when you need server state\n  await collection.utils.refetch()\n}\n```\n\nWhen no user mutation is persisting and no mutation handler is active, the\n`refetch()` promise resolves after every accepted result from that call has been\napplied to Collection rows. This is also the application barrier when the\nresult has no row diff and therefore emits no change event. Rejection is\nfail-fast: with several tracked Queries, one failure may reject the call while\nindependent work remains pending.\n\nWhile a user mutation is persisting or a Collection mutation handler is active,\nnormal application is queued behind that transaction. To avoid a circular wait\nor an unrelated persistence delay, all overlapping `refetch()` calls resolve at\nthe Query fetch boundary. This rule applies whether the mutation or the refetch\nstarts first. It includes calls through the handler parameter, captured or\nexternal Collection references, manual transaction mutation functions, and\n`createOptimisticAction` mutation functions. Code can await the mutation's\n`isPersisted.promise` when it needs the complete transaction and its queued\nCollection application. Utilities that refetch, including `clearError()`,\ninherit the same phase boundary.\n\nThe handler's `collection` parameter and every matching\n`transaction.mutations[n].collection` alias preserve the external Collection\nidentity. Strict equality, `Map`, and `WeakMap` lookups therefore continue to\nwork in shared persistence handlers. Phase tracking changes the refetch wait\nboundary without replacing the Collection, transaction, or mutation objects.\n\nAt the application boundary, `throwOnError` applies to both Query fetch errors\nand errors applying an accepted result to Collection rows. With\n`throwOnError: true`, application failure or cancellation rejects the call;\ncancellation uses an `AbortError`. With `throwOnError: false` (the default),\n`refetch()` returns its Query results instead. At the mutation-phase fetch\nboundary, `throwOnError` applies only to the Query fetch because application\nhappens after that call settles. A later application failure is recorded by the\nCollection's error utilities.\n\nTo skip refetch in v1.0, simply don't call `refetch()`:\n\n```typescript\nonInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n  await api.createTodos(transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.modified))\n\n  // No refetch call = no refetch (v1.0 behavior)\n}\n```\n\n#### When to Skip Refetch\n\nSkip refetching when:\n\n- You're confident the server state exactly matches what you sent (no server-side processing)\n- You're handling state updates through other mechanisms (like WebSockets or direct writes)\n- You want to optimize for fewer network requests\n\n## Utility Methods\n\nThe collection provides these utility methods via `collection.utils`:\n\n- `refetch(opts?)`: Refetch every tracked Query and await the applicable fetch or application boundary\n  - `opts.throwOnError`: Whether Query fetch or Collection application errors reject an application-boundary call (default: `false`); mutation-phase calls cover fetch errors only\n  - Bypasses `enabled: false` to support imperative/manual refetching patterns (similar to hook `refetch()` behavior)\n  - Returns the tracked Queries' `QueryObserverResult` values in tracked-key order\n  - Preserves an `undefined` slot if a tracked Query is removed while the refetch starts\n- `clearError()`: Refetch with errors enabled; retain the recorded error until a successful result applies to the Collection\n  - Uses the same phase-dependent boundary as `refetch()`. During a mutation handler, the promise can resolve at the fetch boundary before the result applies and clears the error.\n\n## Direct Writes\n\nDirect writes are intended for scenarios where the normal query/mutation flow doesn't fit your needs. They write directly to the synced data store and bypass the optimistic update system. Their Query cache behavior depends on the collection's sync mode.\n\n### Understanding the Data Stores\n\nQuery Collections maintain two data stores:\n\n1. **Synced Data Store** - The authoritative state synchronized with the server via `queryFn`\n2. **Optimistic Mutations Store** - Temporary changes that are applied optimistically before server confirmation\n\nNormal collection operations (insert, update, delete) create optimistic mutations that are:\n\n- Applied immediately to the UI\n- Sent to the server via persistence handlers\n- Rolled back automatically if the server request fails\n- Replaced with server data when the query refetches\n\nDirect writes bypass this system entirely and write directly to the synced data store, making them useful for handling real-time updates from alternative sources. Active on-demand queries still refetch so each scoped cache remains authoritative for its own request.\n\n### When to Use Direct Writes\n\nDirect writes should be used when:\n\n- You need to sync real-time updates from WebSockets or server-sent events\n- You're dealing with large datasets where refetching everything is too expensive\n- You receive incremental updates or server-computed field updates\n- You need to implement complex pagination or partial data loading scenarios\n\n### Individual Write Operations\n\n```typescript\n// Insert a new item directly to the synced data store\ntodosCollection.utils.writeInsert({\n  id: \"1\",\n  text: \"Buy milk\",\n  completed: false,\n})\n\n// Update an existing item in the synced data store\ntodosCollection.utils.writeUpdate({ id: \"1\", completed: true })\n\n// Delete an item from the synced data store\ntodosCollection.utils.writeDelete(\"1\")\n\n// Upsert (insert or update) in the synced data store\ntodosCollection.utils.writeUpsert({\n  id: \"1\",\n  text: \"Buy milk\",\n  completed: false,\n})\n```\n\nThese operations:\n\n- Write directly to the synced data store\n- Do NOT create optimistic mutations\n- Return a promise that resolves when the sync commit is accepted, including any configured persistence write. Await it in a mutation handler before returning so the server response is stored before the optimistic state drops.\n- While a mutation handler is running, wait behind it and become visible when its optimistic transaction settles, in the same update that drops its optimistic state\n- In eager mode, update the full-result TanStack Query cache in place without refetching\n- In on-demand mode, refetch active enabled queries and remove inactive or disabled cache entries\n\nThe promise does not wait for on-demand Query revalidation. If a handler returns before it writes the server response, its optimistic state drops and the row shows the previous server value until the response arrives.\nAfter Collection cleanup starts, direct writes fail with `SyncNotInitializedError` until a new sync run starts.\n\n### Batch Operations\n\nThe `writeBatch` method allows you to perform multiple operations atomically. Any write operations called within the callback will be collected and executed as a single transaction:\n\nThe batch includes writes to this Collection only. A synchronous callback may also write to another Collection, but that write has its own completion promise.\n\n```typescript\ntodosCollection.utils.writeBatch(() => {\n  todosCollection.utils.writeInsert({ id: \"1\", text: \"Buy milk\" })\n  todosCollection.utils.writeInsert({ id: \"2\", text: \"Walk dog\" })\n  todosCollection.utils.writeUpdate({ id: \"3\", completed: true })\n  todosCollection.utils.writeDelete(\"4\")\n})\n```\n\nAwait `writeBatch(...)` to wait for the whole sync commit. The callback must be synchronous; the promises returned by writes inside it settle with the batch.\n\n### Real-World Example: WebSocket Integration\n\nThis example assumes the collection stays loaded for the app's lifetime, so the\nlistener can live at module scope.\n\n```typescript\n// Apply real-time changes from a WebSocket as direct writes\nws.on(\"todos:update\", (changes) => {\n  todosCollection.utils.writeBatch(() => {\n    changes.forEach((change) => {\n      switch (change.type) {\n        case \"insert\":\n          todosCollection.utils.writeInsert(change.data)\n          break\n        case \"update\":\n          todosCollection.utils.writeUpdate(change.data)\n          break\n        case \"delete\":\n          todosCollection.utils.writeDelete(change.id)\n          break\n      }\n    })\n  })\n})\n```\n\n- In eager mode, these writes patch the cached query result without a\n  refetch. In on-demand mode, each direct write refetches the active queries.\n  See [Direct Writes and Query Sync](#direct-writes-and-query-sync).\n- A direct write is validated against the synced store after every earlier\n  sync commit is accepted. `writeUpdate` and `writeDelete` reject their\n  promise if the row is not in the synced store, and `writeInsert` rejects if\n  the row is already there. They do not throw. An event can arrive before the\n  first load finishes, or for a row the collection has not loaded. Handle the\n  rejection, or use `writeUpsert` when events carry the full row.\n- In a persisted collection, a direct write can wait for an earlier sync\n  commit's durable write, so its rows can appear after the call returns. Await\n  the returned promise before you read them.\n\nA direct write returns a promise. Await it to know that the write applied, and\ncatch its rejection to handle a validation error:\n\n```typescript\ntry {\n  await todosCollection.utils.writeUpdate({ id: change.id, done: true })\n} catch (error) {\n  if (error instanceof UpdateOperationItemNotFoundError) {\n    // The row is not in the synced store yet. Refetch, or skip this event.\n    await todosCollection.utils.refetch()\n  } else {\n    throw error\n  }\n}\n```\n\nImport `UpdateOperationItemNotFoundError`, `DeleteOperationItemNotFoundError`\nand `DuplicateKeyInBatchError` from `@tanstack/query-db-collection`, and\n`DuplicateKeySyncError` from `@tanstack/db`.\n\n### Example: Incremental Updates\n\nWhen the server returns computed fields (like server-generated IDs or timestamps), you can use direct writes to sync the server response without triggering a full refetch:\n\n```typescript\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: fetchTodos,\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n      const newItems = transaction.mutations.map((m) => m.modified)\n\n      // Send to server and get back items with server-computed fields\n      const serverItems = await api.createTodos(newItems)\n\n      // Sync server-computed fields (like server-generated IDs, timestamps, etc.)\n      // to the collection's synced data store using direct writes\n      await collection.utils.writeBatch(() => {\n        serverItems.forEach((serverItem) => {\n          collection.utils.writeInsert(serverItem)\n        })\n      })\n\n      // No need to refetch - we've already synced the server response via direct writes\n      // (optimistic state is automatically replaced when handler completes)\n      return { refetch: false } // Transitional pre-1.0 opt-out\n    },\n\n    onUpdate: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n      const updates = transaction.mutations.map((m) => ({\n        id: m.key,\n        changes: m.changes,\n      }))\n      const serverItems = await api.updateTodos(updates)\n\n      // Sync server-computed fields from the update response\n      await collection.utils.writeBatch(() => {\n        serverItems.forEach((serverItem) => {\n          collection.utils.writeUpdate(serverItem)\n        })\n      })\n\n      // No refetch needed since we used direct writes\n      return { refetch: false } // Transitional pre-1.0 opt-out\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n// Usage is just like a regular collection\ntodosCollection.insert({ text: \"Buy milk\", completed: false })\n```\n\n### Server pagination with live queries\n\n`useLiveInfiniteQuery` in React, Vue, and Svelte grows a local ordered query\nwindow. It does not run TanStack Query's `InfiniteQueryObserver`. Query\nCollections use `QueryObserver`, so `queryFn` receives\n`meta.loadSubsetOptions`, not `pageParam`.\n\nThe previously ignored `getNextPageParam` option has been removed. Delete it\nfrom your hook config; passing it at runtime now throws a clear error.\n`initialPageParam` labels result pages only. It does not set a remote offset\nor server cursor.\n\nFor server loading, use `syncMode: 'on-demand'` and make `queryFn` fulfill the\nrequested filter, order, offset, and limit. Use a deterministic total order\n(for example, a timestamp followed by a unique ID). The loader may request a\nprefix, a suffix, a tie group, or the full filtered source. A request is not\nnecessarily one UI page: the hook fetches an extra row to determine\n`hasNextPage`. Returning one capped endpoint page can incorrectly make the\nquery appear exhausted even when the server has more rows.\n\n#### Endpoints with fixed-size pages\n\nIf your endpoint uses page numbers, drain enough server pages to fulfill each\nrequest. This example assumes a zero-based page API with a fixed size of 50.\nThe endpoint must apply the supplied filters and sorts **before** pagination,\nkeep a consistent ordered result while its pages are read, and return\n`nextPage: null` only when it has authoritatively exhausted that result.\nThis example uses offset-based pagination. `api.listPosts` translates the full\n`where` expression and `orderBy` options into the endpoint's syntax, and rejects\nunsupported expressions. The separate `cursor` hints are deliberately unused;\ncursor-based adapters must handle those hints alongside `where`, not treat them\nas already included in it. See [QueryFn and Predicate Push-Down](#queryfn-and-predicate-push-down)\nfor translation helpers. Do not drop predicates or filter after paginating:\neither changes the requested window.\n\n```typescript\nimport { createCollection } from '@tanstack/db'\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from '@tanstack/query-db-collection'\n\ntype Post = { id: number; createdAt: number; title: string }\nconst serverPageSize = 50\n\nconst postsCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: ['posts'],\n    queryClient,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    getKey: (post: Post) => post.id,\n    queryFn: async (ctx): Promise<Array<Post>> => {\n      const { where, orderBy, offset = 0, limit } = ctx.meta?.loadSubsetOptions ?? {}\n      const skip = offset % serverPageSize\n      let page: number | null = Math.floor(offset / serverPageSize)\n      const gathered: Array<Post> = []\n\n      while (page !== null && (limit === undefined || gathered.length < skip + limit)) {\n        ctx.signal.throwIfAborted()\n        const response: { rows: Array<Post>; nextPage: number | null } =\n          await api.listPosts({\n            page,\n            pageSize: serverPageSize,\n            where,\n            orderBy,\n            signal: ctx.signal,\n          })\n        gathered.push(...response.rows)\n        page = response.nextPage\n      }\n\n      return gathered.slice(skip, limit === undefined ? undefined : skip + limit)\n    },\n  }),\n)\n\n// React example; the collection protocol is the same for Vue and Svelte.\nconst { data, fetchNextPage, hasNextPage } = useLiveInfiniteQuery(\n  (q) => q.from({ post: postsCollection })\n    .orderBy(({ post }) => post.createdAt)\n    .orderBy(({ post }) => post.id),\n  { pageSize: 20 },\n)\n```\n\nReject failed requests instead of returning partial rows as success. An\nunlimited request must drain until the endpoint reports exhaustion. For an\nendpoint with opaque cursors, use `createCursorPager` as shown below. The hook\ndoes not maintain remote cursor history on its own.\n\nManually appending rows with `writeUpsert` is a separate, lower-level loading\nstrategy. It does not make an eager `queryFn` incremental: a later successful\nrefetch still replaces its complete state and can remove appended rows.\n`staleTime: Infinity` does not prevent explicit refetch or invalidation.\n\n#### Endpoints with opaque cursors\n\nQuery Collections ask for arbitrary windows, such as rows 40–59. An\nopaque-cursor API cannot jump to row 40. It can only fetch the first page, then\nfollow the continuation token returned with each page.\n\n`createCursorPager` bridges those two protocols. It stores complete backend\npages in your existing QueryClient and follows their cursors until it has enough\nrows for the requested `offset` and `limit`. A later request can reuse that\nfresh run of pages and fetch only the missing suffix. The hook still requests\nan extra row to decide `hasNextPage`; the pager just fulfills the resulting\nwindow.\n\n```typescript\nimport { createCollection } from '@tanstack/db'\nimport { createCursorPager, queryCollectionOptions } from '@tanstack/query-db-collection'\n\ntype Post = { id: number; createdAt: number; title: string }\n\nconst postsCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryClient,\n    queryKey: ['posts', 'rows'],\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    getKey: (post: Post) => post.id,\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      const { where, orderBy, offset = 0, limit } = ctx.meta?.loadSubsetOptions ?? {}\n\n      // Translate every filter and sort, or reject unsupported expressions.\n      // This stable, serializable value identifies one ordered result sequence.\n      const request = api.translatePostQuery({ where, orderBy })\n\n      const pager = createCursorPager<Post>({\n        queryClient,\n        queryKey: ['posts', 'cursor-pages', request],\n        staleTime: 60_000,\n        gcTime: 5 * 60_000,\n        fetchPage: async (cursor, signal) => {\n          const response = await api.listPosts({ ...request, cursor, signal })\n\n          // Only null means that the source is exhausted.\n          return { rows: response.items, nextCursor: response.nextCursor ?? null }\n        },\n      })\n\n      return pager.read({ offset, limit }, ctx.signal)\n    },\n  }),\n)\n```\n\n##### Give each result sequence its own key\n\nThe pager's query key identifies one ordered backend result, not one requested\nwindow. Leave `offset` and `limit` out of the key so wider reads can extend the\nsame cached prefix. Include everything that changes the result—such as the\ntenant, source, filters, and order.\n\nKeep row data and cursor pages under sibling keys:\n\n```text\n['posts', 'rows']                         QueryCollection row array\n['posts', 'cursor-pages', request]        backend pages and cursors\n```\n\nThey cannot share a key because the two cache entries have different shapes.\nDo not put cursor pages beneath the row key either: manual collection writes\ntarget entries under the row prefix. A shared resource prefix such as\n`['posts']` still lets you cancel or invalidate both kinds of data together.\n\nThe example creates a small pager object for each `queryFn` call. Those objects\nstill share cached pages and in-flight fetches through QueryClient. Each pager\nserializes its own reads. Retain one pager per result sequence only if calls\nmust also share that queue or you need to call `pager.reset()` later.\n\n##### What happens when the collection asks for rows\n\nFor fresh data, the pager uses every cached page it can and fetches only the\nmissing suffix. `nextCursor: null` is the sole exhaustion signal: a short or\nempty page with another cursor does not end the sequence. An omitted `limit`\ndrains the source; a zero limit performs no fetch. Repeated continuation tokens\nthrow instead of looping forever.\n\nQuery owns freshness, garbage collection, retries, and invalidation. Pass\n`staleTime` and `gcTime` to the pager or let it inherit QueryClient defaults.\nConfigure retries through QueryClient defaults for the page-key prefix;\nimperative Query fetches do not retry by default. The pager keeps every page in\nits original form, so Query defaults for `maxPages` and `select` do not apply.\n\nWhen cached pages are stale, the next read refetches every page loaded so far,\nstarting with the first. It does this even for a shallow window because old\ncontinuation tokens belong to the old sequence. Staleness does not start a\nbackground timer. The page query has no lasting observer, so its `gcTime` may\nexpire while the collection's published rows remain visible.\n\nA failed read rejects rather than returning a partial window. Query keeps the\nlast successful pages, so a later read can retry. Treat their rows as immutable,\njust like other Query data. `read()` returns references from the cached pages,\nnot detached copies, and does not promise the same array or object identity\nacross calls.\n\n##### Refresh or cancel the sequence\n\nAfter a mutation—or whenever you need a forced refresh—cancel the shared\nresource prefix before invalidating it:\n\n```typescript\nawait queryClient.cancelQueries({ queryKey: ['posts'] })\nawait queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['posts'] })\n```\n\nCancelling first stops an old page append from completing after invalidation and\nmarking the old sequence fresh again. It rejects readers waiting on that fetch\nwith an `AbortError` and prevents a late result from entering the cache. The\nbackend must honor the signal if you also want to stop its network work.\n\nAborting the signal passed to `read()` rejects only that reader; it does not\ncancel a page fetch shared with another reader. A cancelling Query refetch moves\nwaiting readers to the replacement fetch. `collection.utils.refetch()` refreshes\nthe row queries but may reuse fresh cursor pages. `pager.reset()` removes one\npager's exact page key and cancels its queued reads, but does not refresh the\ncollection's row query.\n\n##### Backend contract\n\nThe helper handles cursor traversal only. Your adapter must:\n\n- translate every requested `where` and `orderBy`, or reject unsupported\n  expressions;\n- apply filters and ordering before pagination;\n- use a deterministic total order, including a unique tie-breaker;\n- keep that ordered result consistent while its cursor sequence is read; and\n- return `nextCursor: null` only when the result is authoritatively exhausted.\n\n`LoadSubsetOptions.cursor` contains query-expression hints, not the backend's\nopaque token. `createCursorPager` therefore addresses the cursor sequence by\noffset and deliberately does not consume those hints. No client-side pager can\nmake a stable snapshot from an endpoint whose ordering changes between pages;\nthe endpoint must define its own consistency and cursor-expiry rules.\n\n## Important Behaviors\n\n### Full State Sync\n\nThe query collection treats the `queryFn` result as the **complete state** of the collection. This means:\n\n- Items present in the collection but not in the query result will be deleted\n- Items in the query result but not in the collection will be inserted\n- Items present in both will be updated if they differ\n\nThis is important when the same entity type can be loaded from multiple REST\nendpoints. For example, do not point the same Query Collection at\n`/api/documents/preview` for one load and `/api/documents/deleted` for another\nunless each result represents the complete state for that collection\nscope. A narrower endpoint can otherwise remove rows that were loaded from a\ndifferent endpoint.\n\nFor multiple endpoint or subset-loading use cases, choose the pattern that\nmatches your API semantics:\n\n- Use `syncMode: 'on-demand'` when one logical collection can serve different\n  subsets of data. In this mode, query predicates (`where`, `orderBy`, `limit`,\n  and `offset`) are passed to your `queryFn` via `ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions`,\n  letting you translate them into API parameters.\n- Use separate Query Collections when endpoints represent distinct server scopes\n  whose results should not replace each other. Use `unionAll` to combine them\n  into a single query when you need a unified view across endpoints.\n- Use direct writes such as `writeUpsert`/`writeBatch` for lower-level\n  incremental loading when you intentionally want to merge server responses into\n  the synced store yourself.\n\n### Empty Array Behavior\n\nWhen `queryFn` returns an empty array, **all items in the collection will be deleted**. This is because the collection interprets an empty array as \"the server has no items\".\n\n```typescript\n// This will delete all items in the collection\nqueryFn: async () => []\n```\n\n### Handling Partial/Incremental Fetches\n\nSince the query collection expects `queryFn` to return the complete state, you can handle partial fetches by merging new data with existing data:\n\n```typescript\nconst todosCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n    queryFn: async ({ queryKey }) => {\n      // Get existing data from cache\n      const existingData = queryClient.getQueryData(queryKey) || []\n\n      // Fetch only new/updated items (e.g., changes since last sync)\n      const lastSyncTime = localStorage.getItem(\"todos-last-sync\")\n      const newData = await fetch(`/api/todos?since=${lastSyncTime}`).then(\n        (r) => r.json()\n      )\n\n      // Merge new data with existing data\n      const existingMap = new Map(existingData.map((item) => [item.id, item]))\n\n      // Apply updates and additions\n      newData.forEach((item) => {\n        existingMap.set(item.id, item)\n      })\n\n      // Handle deletions if your API provides them\n      if (newData.deletions) {\n        newData.deletions.forEach((id) => existingMap.delete(id))\n      }\n\n      // Update sync time\n      localStorage.setItem(\"todos-last-sync\", new Date().toISOString())\n\n      // Return the complete merged state\n      return Array.from(existingMap.values())\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  })\n)\n```\n\nThis pattern allows you to:\n\n- Fetch only incremental changes from your API\n- Merge those changes with existing data\n- Return the complete state that the collection expects\n- Avoid the performance overhead of fetching all data every time\n\n### Direct Writes and Query Sync\n\nDirect writes update the collection immediately, or when a running mutation handler settles. In eager mode, they also patch the full-result TanStack Query cache in place.\n\nIn on-demand mode, each Query cache entry may represent a different predicate, order, limit, or offset. A full collection snapshot cannot safely replace those scoped results. Direct writes therefore refetch active enabled queries and remove inactive or disabled entries. A successful `queryFn` result remains authoritative and may reconcile or replace a direct write.\n\nTo handle this properly:\n\n1. Use `{ refetch: false }` in persistence handlers to avoid the handler's additional refetch after a direct write. On-demand cache revalidation still runs.\n2. Make sure an on-demand `queryFn` returns the current server result for its pushed-down predicate, order, limit, and offset.\n3. Use eager mode when direct writes must update one complete cached result without a network request.\n\n## Complete Direct Write API Reference\n\nAll direct write methods are available on `collection.utils`:\n\n- `writeInsert(data)`: Insert one or more items directly\n- `writeUpdate(data)`: Update one or more items directly\n- `writeDelete(keys)`: Delete one or more items directly\n- `writeUpsert(data)`: Insert or update one or more items directly\n- `writeBatch(callback)`: Perform multiple operations atomically\n- `refetch(opts?)`: Trigger a refetch of the query\n\n## QueryFn and Predicate Push-Down\n\nWhen using `syncMode: 'on-demand'`, the collection automatically pushes down query predicates (where clauses, orderBy, limit, and offset) to your `queryFn`. This allows you to fetch only the data needed for each specific query, rather than fetching the entire dataset.\n\n### How LoadSubsetOptions Are Passed\n\nLoadSubsetOptions are passed to your `queryFn` via the query context's `meta` property:\n\n```typescript\nqueryFn: async (ctx) => {\n  // Extract LoadSubsetOptions from the context\n  const { limit, offset, where, orderBy } = ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions\n\n  // Use these to fetch only the data you need\n  // - where: filter expression (AST)\n  // - orderBy: sort expression (AST)\n  // - limit: maximum number of rows\n  // - offset: number of rows to skip (for pagination)\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nThe `where` and `orderBy` fields are expression trees (AST - Abstract Syntax Tree) that need to be parsed. TanStack DB provides helper functions to make this easy.\n\n### Expression Helpers\n\n```typescript\nimport {\n  parseWhereExpression,\n  parseOrderByExpression,\n  extractSimpleComparisons,\n  parseLoadSubsetOptions,\n} from '@tanstack/db'\n// Or from '@tanstack/query-db-collection' (re-exported for convenience)\n```\n\nThese helpers allow you to parse expression trees without manually traversing complex AST structures.\n\n### Quick Start: Simple REST API\n\n```typescript\nimport { createCollection } from '@tanstack/react-db'\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from '@tanstack/query-db-collection'\nimport { parseLoadSubsetOptions } from '@tanstack/db'\nimport { QueryClient } from '@tanstack/query-core'\n\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient()\n\nconst productsCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    id: 'products',\n    queryKey: ['products'],\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand', // Enable predicate push-down\n\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => {\n      const { limit, offset, where, orderBy } = ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions\n\n      // Parse the expressions into simple format\n      const parsed = parseLoadSubsetOptions({ where, orderBy, limit })\n\n      // Build query parameters from parsed filters\n      const params = new URLSearchParams()\n\n      // Add filters\n      parsed.filters.forEach(({ field, operator, value }) => {\n        const fieldName = field.join('.')\n        if (operator === 'eq') {\n          params.set(fieldName, String(value))\n        } else if (operator === 'lt') {\n          params.set(`${fieldName}_lt`, String(value))\n        } else if (operator === 'gt') {\n          params.set(`${fieldName}_gt`, String(value))\n        }\n      })\n\n      // Add sorting\n      if (parsed.sorts.length > 0) {\n        const sortParam = parsed.sorts\n          .map(s => `${s.field.join('.')}:${s.direction}`)\n          .join(',')\n        params.set('sort', sortParam)\n      }\n\n      // Add limit\n      if (parsed.limit) {\n        params.set('limit', String(parsed.limit))\n      }\n\n      // Add offset for pagination\n      if (offset) {\n        params.set('offset', String(offset))\n      }\n\n      const response = await fetch(`/api/products?${params}`)\n      return response.json()\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n// Usage with live queries\nimport { createLiveQueryCollection } from '@tanstack/react-db'\nimport { eq, lt, and } from '@tanstack/db'\n\nconst affordableElectronics = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q.from({ product: productsCollection })\n     .where(({ product }) => and(\n       eq(product.category, 'electronics'),\n       lt(product.price, 100)\n     ))\n     .orderBy(({ product }) => product.price, 'asc')\n     .limit(10)\n     .select(({ product }) => product)\n})\n\n// This triggers a queryFn call with:\n// GET /api/products?category=electronics&price_lt=100&sort=price:asc&limit=10\n// When paginating, offset is included: &offset=20\n```\n\n### Custom Handlers for Complex APIs\n\nFor APIs with specific formats, use custom handlers:\n\n```typescript\nqueryFn: async (ctx) => {\n  const { where, orderBy, limit } = ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions\n\n  // Use custom handlers to match your API's format\n  const filters = parseWhereExpression(where, {\n    handlers: {\n      eq: (field, value) => ({\n        field: field.join('.'),\n        op: 'equals',\n        value\n      }),\n      lt: (field, value) => ({\n        field: field.join('.'),\n        op: 'lessThan',\n        value\n      }),\n      and: (...conditions) => ({\n        operator: 'AND',\n        conditions\n      }),\n      or: (...conditions) => ({\n        operator: 'OR',\n        conditions\n      }),\n    }\n  })\n\n  const sorts = parseOrderByExpression(orderBy)\n\n  return api.query({\n    filters,\n    sort: sorts.map(s => ({\n      field: s.field.join('.'),\n      order: s.direction.toUpperCase()\n    })),\n    limit\n  })\n}\n```\n\n### GraphQL Example\n\n```typescript\nqueryFn: async (ctx) => {\n  const { where, orderBy, limit } = ctx.meta.loadSubsetOptions\n\n  // Convert to a GraphQL where clause format\n  const whereClause = parseWhereExpression(where, {\n    handlers: {\n      eq: (field, value) => ({\n        [field.join('_')]: { _eq: value }\n      }),\n      lt: (field, value) => ({\n        [field.join('_')]: { _lt: value }\n      }),\n      and: (...conditions) => ({ _and: conditions }),\n      or: (...conditions) => ({ _or: conditions }),\n    }\n  })\n\n  // Convert to a GraphQL order_by format\n  const sorts = parseOrderByExpression(orderBy)\n  const orderByClause = sorts.map(s => ({\n    [s.field.join('_')]: s.direction\n  }))\n\n  const { data } = await graphqlClient.query({\n    query: gql`\n      query GetProducts($where: product_bool_exp, $orderBy: [product_order_by!], $limit: Int) {\n        product(where: $where, order_by: $orderBy, limit: $limit) {\n          id\n          name\n          category\n          price\n        }\n      }\n    `,\n    variables: {\n      where: whereClause,\n      orderBy: orderByClause,\n      limit\n    }\n  })\n\n  return data.product\n}\n```\n\n### Expression Helper API Reference\n\n#### `parseLoadSubsetOptions(options)`\n\nConvenience function that parses all LoadSubsetOptions at once. Good for simple use cases.\n\n```typescript\nconst { filters, sorts, limit, offset } = parseLoadSubsetOptions(ctx.meta?.loadSubsetOptions)\n// filters: [{ field: ['category'], operator: 'eq', value: 'electronics' }]\n// sorts: [{ field: ['price'], direction: 'asc', nulls: 'last' }]\n// limit: 10\n// offset: 20 (for pagination)\n```\n\n#### `parseWhereExpression(expr, options)`\n\nParses a WHERE expression using custom handlers for each operator. Use this for complete control over the output format.\n\n```typescript\nconst filters = parseWhereExpression(where, {\n  handlers: {\n    eq: (field, value) => ({ [field.join('.')]: value }),\n    lt: (field, value) => ({ [`${field.join('.')}_lt`]: value }),\n    and: (...filters) => Object.assign({}, ...filters)\n  },\n  onUnknownOperator: (operator, args) => {\n    console.warn(`Unsupported operator: ${operator}`)\n    return null\n  }\n})\n```\n\n#### `parseOrderByExpression(orderBy)`\n\nParses an ORDER BY expression into a simple array.\n\n```typescript\nconst sorts = parseOrderByExpression(orderBy)\n// Returns: [{ field: ['price'], direction: 'asc', nulls: 'last' }]\n```\n\n#### `extractSimpleComparisons(expr)`\n\nExtracts simple AND-ed comparisons from a WHERE expression. Note: Only works for simple AND conditions.\n\n```typescript\nconst comparisons = extractSimpleComparisons(where)\n// Returns: [\n//   { field: ['category'], operator: 'eq', value: 'electronics' },\n//   { field: ['price'], operator: 'lt', value: 100 }\n// ]\n```\n\n### Supported Operators\n\n- `eq` - Equality (=)\n- `gt` - Greater than (>)\n- `gte` - Greater than or equal (>=)\n- `lt` - Less than (<)\n- `lte` - Less than or equal (<=)\n- `and` - Logical AND\n- `or` - Logical OR\n- `in` - IN clause\n\n### Using Query Key Builders\n\nWith `syncMode: 'on-demand'`, a static key such as `queryKey: ['products']`\nautomatically includes the subset demand identity. Filters, ordering, limits,\noffsets, and cursor hints distinguish cache entries when they change the\nrequested data. Prefer a static key when you do not need a custom key structure.\n\nA function-based `queryKey` replaces that automatic key construction. Include\nall options that change the requested data; omitting `offset`, for example,\ncan make different windows share a cache entry. Use `getLoadSubsetDemandKey`\nfrom `@tanstack/db` to retain the same subset identity as a static key, including\n`limit: 0`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { getLoadSubsetDemandKey } from '@tanstack/db'\n\nconst productsCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    id: 'products',\n    queryKey: (opts) => {\n      const demandKey = getLoadSubsetDemandKey(opts)\n      return demandKey === undefined ? ['products'] : ['products', demandKey]\n    },\n    queryClient,\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    queryFn: async (ctx) => { /* ... */ },\n  })\n)\n```\n\n#### Query Key Prefix Convention\n\nWhen using a function-based `queryKey`, all derived keys **must extend the base key as a prefix**. The base key is what your function returns when called with no options (`queryKey({})`).\n\nTanStack Query uses prefix matching for cache operations internally. The query collection relies on this to find all cache entries belonging to a collection — including stale entries from destroyed query observers that are still held in cache due to `gcTime`. If derived keys don't share the base prefix, cache updates may silently miss entries, leading to stale data.\n\nThe example above returns `['products']` for `queryKey({})` and keeps it as\nthe prefix of every derived key. Avoid changing the prefix for subset demands:\n\n```typescript\n// ❌ Wrong: base key ['products-all'] is NOT a prefix of ['products-filtered', ...]\nqueryKey: (opts) => {\n  const demandKey = getLoadSubsetDemandKey(opts)\n  return demandKey === undefined\n    ? ['products-all']\n    : ['products-filtered', demandKey]\n}\n```\n\n### Tips\n\n1. **Start with `parseLoadSubsetOptions`** for simple use cases\n2. **Use custom handlers** via `parseWhereExpression` for APIs with specific formats\n3. **Handle unsupported operators** with the `onUnknownOperator` callback\n4. **Log parsed results** during development to verify correctness\n"}
